      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>IvIcDOUGALL'S Pah* SELF—RAISING FLOUR THITZT. The Examinek Saturday, December 12, 1908 WOMAN'S REALM. !SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd-George and Women's Suffrage.—Violent scenes characterised the Women's Liberal Federation. Their aim is to secure Women's enfranceisement by constitutional methods, and in spite of the free fights and violent scuttles, for which the militant suffragists were responsible, Mr. Lloyd-George succeeded in obtaining a hearing, and repeated the undertaking given by Mr. Asquith last May. This is to the effect that before the dissolution, the government will introduce an Electoral Reform Bill, and if a majority of the House of Commons decides in favour of a women's suffrage clause, the government would accept the decision and put it in the Bill. That is a clear and definite promise, and as over 400 members of the House of Commons are pledged to women's enfranchisements there seems little doubt within two or three years, women will have the Parliamentary vote. Even that prospect however does not satisfy the militant suffragists, who apparently love heroes more dearly than the gaining of their cause. The Saloon Smasber.—This world would be the poorer without its enthusiasts, one of whom is Mrs. Carry A. Nation, the American temperance reformer. She has recently started a campaign in Scotland under the anspices of the Scottish Prohibitionists, and is known in her own country as the " bar smasher." Just 89 the militant suffragist has a great contempt for con- stitutional methods, so Mrs. Nation thinks nothing of ordinary temperance organisations, and she has been in prison thirty-three times for what she practically calls " Hatchetation." That is the breaking up of drinking bars with an axe, at.a the emblem of her mission is a little badge hatchet whimi algtributes among her -followers. Nation is a white-haired benevolent-looking lady of three score years, and her battle cry is " Down with the Liquor Saloon." Miss RO9B. N. Carey's Works.—Boots are always acceptable as Christmas presents, and unlike so many other gifts possess the undoubted merit of durability. When considering girls of the grown-up stage, it would be a difficult matter to find anything more suitable than Miss Carey's novels, the latest of which bears the title "The Sunny Side of the Hill" (Macmillan). All the works of this charming authoress hold ones's keenest interest, yet for the most part they are just simple stories of simple home life. The girl who possesses Miss Carey's works complete, has not only pleasure laid up for her now, but in the years to come as, with their many beautiful characters, exquisitely drawn, they never fail to delight us all, whatever the number of our years may be. MADGE.</p>
        <p>LIFTING MAGNETS.</p>
        <p>Lifting magnets are being used quite exten- sively in some of the large machine-tool plants of the Middle West, says-the Scientific American. These magnets are not only em- ployed for handling iron and steel cxtstings, but also for cleaning up the small particles of metal from the floor or even from the yard around the plant. They are suspended from locomotive cranes and moved about the yard close to the ground. The amount of steel and iron they collect is astonishing. Often pieces that have mysteriously disappeared are resur- rected by. the magnet, sometimes shewing that they were purposely buried to hide mis- takes of the employees. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>FOIBLES OF FAT FOLKS. SOMETHING TO sEr THEM RIGHT.</head>
        <p>FOIBLES OF FAT FOLKS. SOMETHING TO sEr THEM RIGHT.</p>
        <p>Too many fat folks seem to think that by going without a rational quantity of food they can cure the disease of obesity. Others pin their faith to exercis- ing, or body-contortion, which, the very stout, is attended with considerable danger. A little common- sense reasoning should convince any man or woman that merely weakening the body for a time can be no true cure for over-stoutness. Fortunately, science has recently provided the world with a remedy which goes to the root of the evil and cures the disease itself. The ingzedients are here given :—One half-ounce of Marmola, one ounce of Glycyrrhiza 8.P., one ounce of pure Glycerine B.P. Mix these with noz of Pepper- mint Water (or get your chemist to do the mixing), and take two teaspoonfuls after each meal and at bed- time. At the same time, feed up well, and don't fatigue yourself beyond ordinary need. In a very short time the change will be wonderful not only in the body proportions but in health and strength, ease and comfort, freshness of complexion, nervous energy. If the above prescription does not lead to a very satisfactory cure of over-fatness nothing will.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>UNEMPLOYMENT IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>UNEMPLOYMENT IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Working of the Labour Bureau.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Fargher, labour bureau inquiry officer, presents the following report :—During November 189 fresh applicants registered their names for employment, as compared with 152 during November, 1907. Sixty-two situations were offered by employers, and the number of situations filled through the bureau office was as follows :-14 men, 6 youths, 7 women,l2 girls, a total of 39, as compared with 50 during the same period of last year. There were now on the register out of employ- ment 374 persons in the following trades :—Building trade, 17 ; iron and wire workers, 71 ; horsemen, 6 ; clerks, 6 ; wood working and furniture trade, 5; general labourers, 77 ; miscellaneous other occupations for men, 7 ; youths under eighteen years old, 58 ; woman for charring and daily work, 67 ; miscellaneous other occupations for women, 29 ; girls under 12 years old, 81. Employment in November was on the whole much about the same as in October, except the building trade which showed a decline and was considered to be much worse than a year ago. It is believed that there will not be much change until after the Christmas holidays and most of those who are now out of employment will have to remain out another month. The committee are anxious to do what they can to relieve known cases of distress and have sent a resolution to the Town , Council to that effect. The Council appointed a sub- committee to meet them to consider work of utility, and both committees are anxious that something may be done. During November efforts were also made by the Distress Committee to ascertain the fullest possible, information as to the extent of unemployment in the borough to enable them to efficiency deal with the , same, but, owing probably to the apathy of many unemployed persons in not registering their names at the Committee's Labour Bureau Office, it would appear that distress is not so acute in Warrington as in some towns, and consequently Warrington does seem to be in more favourable circumstances than some of the large centres. But some of our townspeople who take a keen interest in the question are inclined to think that unemployment and short-time are much more extensive than generally supposed by those who are in more favourable circumstances. As the Labour Bureau is the Distress Committee's chief means of obtaining information to enable them to obtain a grant from Mr. John Burns's fund for relief works, it is absolutely essential that all unemployed persons of reliable character such as will bear investigation) should register their names without delay both in their own interest and especially in the interest of:those who have big families and little or no food to provide for their needs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
