      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>TAFFETAS, HATS, AND RUFFLES.</head>
        <p>TAFFETAS, HATS, AND RUFFLES.</p>
        <p>Some very dainty sets of hat and ruffle are made of white or coloured taUctzte edged with fur. One of these has the silk arranged in large, wide loops all departing from a com- mon centre over the left ear, and appa:rently fastened there with an oxydised buckle. The ruffle is also in white silk bordered with a narrow line of sable, and tied with brown satin ribbons. Other sets consist of toque and stole. One of theee is in a soft tone of water-blue, bordered with brown feathers, the toque having a little of the feather intro- duced. In black silk, these are very neat and becoming. declares the Globe, especially when edged with mink or marten. Grey silk with ermin or beaver is rather more an- gerous as a neighbour for the complexion on a cold, raw day. But youth and beauty triumph over such considerations as this.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>SMART BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>A SMART BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>The dressy blouse shewn iri our sketch is one that could be carried out in a variety of materials such as net or silk, or we would suggest a pretty soft shade of blue crepe-de- chine. The blouse is cut with a square yoke, the fulness ,being gathered into a narrow band, which is hidden beneath the square yoke of wide lace insertion. The lace is only attached at the top edge, the lower edge being</p>
        <p>left to hang loose and the corners v-eighted with small blue silk taf.;seis. Inside this square of lace there is' another band of lace arranged in a V-shape, the corners being filled in with little pieces of tuckc.,d cOpo-de-chine, and the guimpe and collar arc of -finely-tucked tulle. The sleeves are i.ranged in large tucks, a small blue silk tassel being attached to the edge of each tuck.</p>
        <p>"TEE CLEVEREST WOMAN IN TUE WORLD:" Such is the magnificent title that has been bestowed upon Madame Curie, the brilliant Lady who has recently been prometed to the Professorship of General Physics at the Sor- bonne. As the joint db.coverer with her late husband of radium. Madame Curie has earned the graitude of scienti..sts all the world over, and her labest honour is but one more example of the esteem in which she is held in her adopted country. Maclaine Curie was torn in Warsaw, and became interested in scientific subjects at an • caslyge. For some time she was a, governess, in The family of •a Warsaw merchant, but afti,,t..wards, on going to Paris, she metrl, the acquaintance of M. Curie, whose subsequent marriage with her proved of ,such great benefit to the scien- tific world. Apart from her extensive know ledge of physical subjects, Madame Curie is a lecturer sad public speaker of remarltable power, and she has contributed some valuable writings to the liferature of science. TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>BEEF STEAK PlE.—Thico two pounds of raw beef, cut it into thin slice's, cbout 2. long and lin. /wide. Season them with black pepper and salt and a little chopped onion and parsley. Roll them up and dust them with flour. Cut half an ex kidney into :thin aim* Aea.son in the some • way, also some cznall pieces of b:•_!ef fat. Fill n pie-dish with all thee, and cover with a good gravy.- Put an edge of puff .pa.3try rmuid the dish, and on this- a-cover of pastry to fit the whole pie. Garnish with lcaves of pastry and bruch over with -baaten-up e. Bake in a hot oven for two and a-half hours. When the pastry is cooK.ct, Keep it caverect until a piece or kitchen paper, and stand the dish in a tin containing boiling water, and return it to the even. The meat will then be tender, as it will cook slowly,</p>
