      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>TUNGSTEN-FILAME,NT LAMPS.</head>
        <p>TUNGSTEN-FILAME,NT LAMPS.</p>
        <p>The substitution of tungsten for carbon ae the filament of the electric lamp marked a considerable improvement in the light. And the tungsten filament %sad, it appears, may be improved by judicious mixture. Thus, says the Globe, if thorium oxide is mixed with the tungsten *ha rPeiat is increased, and shorter filaments can be used. Thus it is found that an addition of 20 per cent. of thorium oxide increases the resistance of the filament 50 per cent. Up to 50 per cent. of thorium may be used, but a larger quantity renders the filament too brittle. It is also found to be advantageous to add an alloy of cadium, bismuth, and mercury to the tang- ' sten-thorium mixture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>WARM AIR OVER POLES.</head>
        <p>WARM AIR OVER POLES.</p>
        <p>By means of kites and sounding balloons carrying meteorological instruments our knowledge of the higher atmosphere has been greatly increased. Heights of over eight miles have thus been investigated. As a re- sult the familiar assumption that a fall of ldeg. Fahrenheit takes place for every 300 ft. ascent must be modified. At the height of eight miles the thermometer ceases to fall, and may even begin to rise. And a French meteorologist, Mr. Tisseranc de Bort, claims that at certain 'levels above the Poles the air is warmer than at the same height over the Equator. This may be compared with the fact that the depths of the ocean at the Equator have a temperature about freezing paint. And an atmospheric circulation similar to the oceanic movements which produce the latter may be suggested as an explanation of the former.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>USE OF GLASS SPONGES.</head>
        <p>USE OF GLASS SPONGES.</p>
        <p>Under the form of the. exquisitely beautiful Venus' Flower-basket and the Bird's-nest sponge, the silicious or glassy sponges are familiar objects in many museums. In them the familiar soft substance of the sponge of domestic life is replaced by network of sili- cious fibres. They are found in the tropical Pacific Ocean from the Fuji Yams region to the Indian Ocean. What asbestos is to Europe such is the fibre of the glass sponges to Asia. It is used for making chemical filter cloths, candle - shades, fire - proof cur- tains, lamp globes and shades, ecc. The beautiful " silk-gauze zephyr-cloths " of the Chinese are made from it. It has been sug- gested as a suitable material for incandes- cent gas mantles. The shorter fibres and debris are used as insulators for steam pipes,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>FOSSIL EGGS.</head>
        <p>FOSSIL EGGS.</p>
        <p>A note in the Scientific American calls at- tention to an interesting discovery of fossil eggs. These were found in a mine at Cop- pereid, Nevada, and some of the eggs are de- scribed as being 'as large as a men's head. They had apparently been buried to a depth of 7,000 ft., and their content had been re- placed by mineral matter. Prestwick, in hie text-book of Geology, mentions the occur- rence of fossil eggs in this country. These are found in the -rocks of the Great Oolite near Cirenceater, and are about the size of pigeons' egge. They are supposed to belong to the reptile Teleosaurus, and their contents have been replaced by calcite. WIRELESS TIME-SIGNALS.</p>
        <p>From time to time efforts have been made to use wireless telegraphy for distributing Lime signals from a central clock to the elec- trically- controlled clocks over a wide area, but these efforts have been unsuccessful be- cause of the disturbances due to other wire- less systems, as well as atmospheric electric waves. However, an Austrian inventor ap- pears to have solved the problem quite ingeni- cusly. His central clock sends a signal only once every sixty seconds, and the clocks to be controlled are provided with apparatus which will receive the Hertzian waves for that second only, while for the remaining fifty-nine they are unaffected by any Hertzian waves. In case of a disturbance during the one second when they are in circuit with the receiver the disturbing wave may set the clock incorrectly and for the next minute it will be a fraction of a second fast or slow. However, this inaccuracy will be corrected by the master clock a minute later.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WHAT IS LIFE?</head>
        <p>WHAT IS LIFE?</p>
        <p>We know neither what Life is, . nor what Death is, nor how nor why the one passes into the other. This, says the Hospital, is not for want of guessing, for many centuries and much hard thinking have been devoted to this business in the realm of pure speculation. But these speculations have not advanced us a whit, and even now, when the sacrosanct problem of Life has long since been im- pounded by biologists and submitted to the cold contemplation of science, we are forced to confess that the secret remains remarkably well hidden. Though much has been learnt of ! the processes attending Life, and of the con- ditions governing. its contintiance or c6nclu- hion, what in essence it is, how it begins, and what determines the gradual failure which we call senescence, on these matters we are still pretty thoroughly in ! the dark. " Radiobes," the putative examples of a spontancoa 3 ! generation under the ! influence of radium emanations, have had their day, and have left us what we were, unexplained examples of the first and greatest mystery that man has encountered—Life.</p>
        <p>MECHANICS OF OVERTHRUSTS. In order to explain the complicated, .geo- logy of the north-west Highlands, and other regions, geologists have suggested- large over- thrust faults. They, ..believe that •great masses of underlying rocks have been brought up and thrust along, often for many miles, aver the younger. In - the Geological Magazine Mr. T. Mellard Reade calls attention to the Mechanical difficulties in the conception .of, such earth movement . Some of the enor- mous overthrusts • involve movements of nearly 100 miles. "'Would it not require," asks Mr. Reade, "an incalculable 'force to thrust the upper block over the lower, even with a clean- fractured bed to move on?" ff the mass to be moved 100 miles ,was 100 I miles long we would have, a block of strata 10,000 miles area to b 3 lifted and moved along. " I venture to think," says Mr. ,Reade, "that no force applied in any of the mechanical ways known to us in Nature would move such a mass, be it ever so Ml- justed in thickness to . the purpose, even if 3upplernented with a lubricant generously applied to the thrust-plane."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>SUN-SPOTS AS MAGNETIC FIELDS.</head>
        <p>SUN-SPOTS AS MAGNETIC FIELDS.</p>
        <p>-- Upon the observations of Professor Hale, made at Mount Wilson, California, on the double lines in sun-spot • spectra, Professor Zeeman bases a theory that sun-spots are I strong magnetic fields,. The (=zee of light in a magnetic field emits two rays circularly polarised in opposite directions and parallel to the lines of magnetic force, according to Professor Zee.man's experiments. The sun- snot lines photographed by Profeegor Hale are identical in character with these lines. Hence Professor Zeeman's conclusion. To produce the actual phenomena observed on the sun would require " a current of about 5,000 ' amperes. The theory throws a great light upon meteorolc gical and terrestrial, magnetic phenomena, affording, as it does, some reason for the perturbations observed in the electric and magnetic equilibrium of our earth and RS .atmcspliere. In, order to account for the force which converts a sun- spot into a mag,nctic field, Professor Hale as- sumes a segregnarion or pcsitivety or nega- tively electrified particles caught in the. Stream of -solar vortex, co as to give rise to lines of magnetic force at right angles to the place of the swirl.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CP" Fa</head>
        <p>CP" Fa</p>
        <p>Quality,</p>
        <p>Flavour</p>
        <p>Digestibility</p>
        <p>no rival.</p>
        <p>• . - • In • , . • '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Creation of Dukes.—The King we know on the poet's a..thority " can mak' a I.clted knight, a marquis, duke, and a' that," but if the Horse of Lords reforms itself in the way proposed in the Committees' scheme, , new peerages will be ew and far between in the future. The reign of Queen Victoria was the most prolific in the creation of new peerages, although there were few , of high rank amongst them. Apart from Royal dukes, and the special circumstances attending the creation of , the Dukedom of Fife, Queen Victoria only made two 1 dukes, one the Marquis of Westminster, and the other Lord Ahercorn, who {NrIS made an Irish duke. Queen , Victoria's predecessor, William IV., created two dukes, those of Sutherland and Cie% eland, whilst George IV. made but. one, the Duke of Buckingham, although a Prince Regent it wee his privilege to give Wellington his dukedom. Ashi</p>
        <p>—The Kin;</p>
        <p>snnd Qneen</p>
        <p>Royal Visit to Dorsets.. re.— —' _ bave been on n. visit to the Earl and Countess of Ilchester at Melbury this week, the Countess being the daughter of one of their Majesties oldest friends, the Marchioness of Londonderry. The Royal visit was a private one, without any public function, and there was a slight change of plans' the King deciding to break the journey between Sandringham and Dorsetshire by a short stay in London. Altogether, their Majesties spend a good deal of time in the railway train, and one is apt to wonder whether they find that the devotion of so much time to travelling interferes with the ordinary I comfort of human existence. It would be interesting to have the views of the Royal Family on the subject. Queen Alexandra has devoted to charity her charming work reproducing photographs from her came, and if I one might venture with great submission to offer a ' suggestion, there seem to be possibilities for a collec- tion of articles by members of the Royal Family on the art of beguiling the tedium of a railway journey. A Society Wedding.—The wedding on Monday of the Hon. George Lambton, and Miss Cecily Horner was quite a "big affair "in Society circles, and an immense number of beautiful and costly wedding gifts was showered upon bride and bridegroom. Mr. George: Lambton to a younger brother of the Earl of Durham, belongs to the ultra-smart racing set and has a house at Newmarket. The bride is the eldest daughter of Sir John and Lady Horner. Tall, and with perfect features and complexion, she has a remarkably distinctive style of beauty, and is a gifted amateur artist. Her younger sister was married last year to Mr. Raymond Asquith, ' eon of the Prime Minister. It is the custom of the ; brides of the Horner family to wear at their weddings' the historic veil which was made by anus for Mary I Queen of Scots., when she was a prisoner at Forheringny I Castle. If is of deep cream lace of exquisitive work- reanship, and was worn by Miss Cecily Horner over a beautiful wedding gown of soft satin. A Favourite Stone.—Amethysts are in great favour among the ladies of our Roylkl Family, who not only' frequently wear the stones themselves, but select them , for gilts. The Duchess of Connaught, following in the 1 Queen's footsteps, frequently bestows amethysts upon ' prospective brides, as in the care of Miss Cecily 1 Monier, who received front Her Royal Highness a beautiful circlet of amethysts, set with diamonds, Many Society vromen,in addition to necklaces,pendants, bracelets, and earrings, of these stones, are wearing rings containing a large square or oblong amethyst, set round with diamonds, or pearls. Amethysts look wonderfully well upon a pretty dainty hand, a fact well-known to our great grandmothers, who were great lovers of amethyst rings. ' Evening Muffs,—The furriers surely ought not to complain of bad times, for the fashionable woman, in addition to 'wearing vast quantities of fur during the day time, has now decided to carry a huge flat muff in the evening. Needless to say many of these " full dress " muffs are truly exquisite creations. Ermine leads, of course, but sable and chinchilla are in evidence, clusters of flowers, jewels, soft delicate hut rich fabrics, tails, etc.. being requisitioned as trimmings. About Tea GONTIIB.—In the world of tea gowns the latest notion is to have these dainty garments built of semi-transparent fabric, in mole colour, with a pale pink, soft green-hlue or mauve lining. The effect is charming, and infinitely more becoming than a mass of colour could possibly be, In the matter of sleeves, these are no longer of elbow length, the three-quarter and even full-length varieties beingpreferred by leading modistes.</p>
        <p>Origin of Thimbles.— ' Man for the field, woman for the hearth; Man for the sword, for the needle she."— So runs an old rhs me, which is recalled by the fact that it is just three hundred years ago that thimbles were first introduced into this country. Probably few house- wives who have placed a silver thimble in the Christmas plum pudding are aware of the fact or that the name is supposed to have been derived from " thumbell," having been first worn on the thumb am the toilerrnakere' substitute for a thimble still is. We are indebted to the Dutch for the thimble as we know it and it is said to have been first manufactured in Britain by John Lofting at Idington in the year 1603.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ut • ns</head>
        <p>ut • ns</p>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES.</p>
        <p>By Veronica.</p>
        <p>SEASONABLE WORE.</p>
        <p>Everyone who intends to make his own Christmas and New Year cards by his own photographic work should be very busy now, and some of my readers may be interested in the following outline hints, quoted from the current number of "The Photographic Monthly.,' PHOTOGRAPHIC CHRISTMAS CARDS. Numerous are the facilities for making photographic Christmas Cards, yet It is surprising how few take advantage of them even amongst the experienced camera workers. Some of the methods are as follows: ' CHRISTMAS CARDS AND FOLDERS. supplied with dainty designs and with suitable greetings I thereon ars obtainable in great variety at almost any dealers in both slip-in and the paste-down kind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ADHESIVE MOTTOES &amp;Nn SEALS</head>
        <p>ADHESIVE MOTTOES &amp;Nn SEALS</p>
        <p>By this method use can be made of any of our photo- grams that we already have mounted. These mottoes are made of skeleton letters in gilt paper with the under side gummed, and are thus very easily fixed to any mounts. The seals are made to represent seals in red wax, and are affixed by means of gum, looking very effective when used on dark mounts. PRINTING METHODS. - -</p>
        <p>The gaslight, bromide' i and print-out postcard with design of a seasonable kind on the front. The double- printing method in which a negative with fancy design is used. This is not used quite so much as the others ; no doubt because the idea of the double printing frightens many from attempting it, but this should not be so, for with a little care and perseverence it is easily managed. The fancy-design negatives which are usually on films are to be obtained from most dealers. Or one may produce a suitable negative oneself and most likely with better result, for the reason that you then have a different subject from those that anyone else can buy. There are many ways to produce these negatives. One simple yet effective way is as follows :—Obtain a large sheet of brown paper if nothing else is available to make a background. Get rid of any creases ;todo ' this, well damp the paper with a wet sponge or cloth, then tack it up against a wall, floor, or other flat surface, and leave to dry. The question then arises where the fix the background. Unless you have a very well lighted room where you can obtain a good supply of top light, it will be best to fix it out of doors against the side of the house, or,better stillaigainst some wooden structure. I,llen this is arranged, got an empty picture frame, of fairly wide moulding for preference; hang this up on the background, and add decoration according to taste, by arranging flowers, grasses, evergreens, etc. A suitable greeting motto may be put in with chalk or white paint. The best effect is gained by screening some of the light that falls on the subject so that the frame and accessories throw a certain amount of shadow on the background. When all is arranged to your satisfaction, bring out your camera and take your photogram. When the negative is dry, block out with some opabue print all the space within the frame, or else carefully cut a piece of black paper to fill the space exactly, and stick this on the negative so that when printing, the inside of the frame remains quite , white. An opaque mask is also required for use with , the negatives that you will be printing from in combina- tion. An easy way to make this is to make a P.O.P. print from the fancy negative, then without fixing it cut out the inside of the frame, and allow the mask that remains to blacken by exposure to daylight. By the use of this double-printing method, single figures out of groups or choice little bits out of larger pictures can be made into charming Christmas cards. MOSIACS. Here again the brown paper will be found nseful. If a few photograrns, say scones of a holiday or other pleasant reminiscence are tastefully mounted on one sheet with a decorative line work to link them together and then reduced down on to a small plate, you have another interesting greeting card.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>ORIGINAL NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>ORIGINAL NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The original idea is left for each to select for him or herself ; there are so many devices that can easily be arranged if only a little time is spent in thinking out something different from what everyone else is doing. The more original the idea the more likely it is to please and interest your friends. Cards that you have made yourself have a touch of personality that is very much wanting in the ordinary commercial greeting card.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>ENLARGEMENTS WITH AN ORDINARY CAMERA.</head>
        <p>ENLARGEMENTS WITH AN ORDINARY CAMERA.</p>
        <p>A good many photographers—but not all—realise that ' things can be copied the same size as the original in many ordinary cameras, but few realise that with very little adaptation the camera can be made to photograph things much larger than life-size. An article on this subject, with many convincing and very interesting illustrations, appeared in the December issue of "The Photographic Monthly," from the pen of Mr. T. U. Crabtree. One of his most effective pictures is of a common house-fly, photographed to a leg-stretch of over two inches, while enlargements of parts of engravings, coins, fern-fronds, etc., are suggested as other examples of useful work. The principle adopted is the use of " supplementary " lenses, which can be bought very cheaply indeed, and the article opens a new, attractive field.</p>
      </div>
