      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 12, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 12, 1908.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>The Foreign Missionary Anniversary was celebrated at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church last week end. The Rev. T. walwyn Nernick, M.A., and the Rev. E. Blanshard Keeling were the preachers, morning and evening respectively. Fairly good congregations assembled and a marked impression was made upon them by the rev, gentlemen's entreaties. " God is a Spirit " was sung as a quartette by Misses Evans, Leicester and Messrs. Tynan and Dutton. On Monday evening the Rev. James Lewis of Southport, and formerly a missionary in Negapatan, India, addressed a public meeting in the church. Dr. Ferguson presided over a good audience, and was supported by the Rev. E. B. Keeling. The Rev. E. BLANSIIARD KEELING presented the report of the circuit in relation to foreign missions. The slim raised last year in the circuit for this good cause was £285 3s. 83. To that amount Bewsey-road had only contributed £2O 15s. 4d., which was a decrease of £l2 3s. on the year 1906. Ile hoped there would be considerable improvement this year. The CEIAIRM AN said there were three reisons why they should support foreign missions. The first was because it was the Master's command ; the second was to maintain the brotherhood ; the third was for their own sakes. He would take the third reason first. They had all heard, more or less, about what was called the I yellow peril. He did not know whether there was auy ground for fear on that subject or not. But, at any rate, they had been told so by men who ought to know. What did this mean ? It meant that millions of yellow men realised the power that lay in numbers, and that they in Europe could be heaped away by them. The only way to touch these people was through the love of Christ and then all would be well. In explaining the second reason he said they could only get the true brotherhood of mankind through Christ. They could fall in with his first reason by their sympathies, and by their prayers and pecuniary help for the men who went out. (Applause). Rev. JAMES LEWIS, in the course of his addre;s, said he wanted the intelligence and the love of people in the work of Foreign Missions. The Kingdom of God was effected by human minds, human hearts and human wills. They would be able to get the money if they could only get the intelligence and sympathy of the people. What was the instinct that arose in the souls of those who were converted when they knew and felt that God was their Father and Christ their Saviour? What was their feeling when they knew they were forgiven ? Did not their hearts heave for the men and women who did not know Christ? Did they not feel related to their fellow man as they did to God ? The nigger had as much claim upon Christ as they had ; Jesus Christ was not an Englishman and every Englishman who became Christ had the responsibility towards all the rest. He (the speaker) had had 5 or G years in India and brought his wife home in a stretcher, so he knew of the hardships of missionary work. But he would rather be speaking to heathens, than speaking there that night. He did not know whv,and he did not care; but it was true. What a task God had given them in the East and in the Empire ! They would never do proper mission work if they were afraid of any man or of any peril. And yet they need not be afraid of it ; for they were all in the hands of God. If 40,000,000 Saps could paralyze the greatest power in the world—Russia—what would happen in 40 years from now? 400,000,000 of Chinese had for 1,600 years been the leaders of the Japanese. There were at the present time two millions of Chinese students studying European methods, and other masses of students were also studying other things for their welfare. The Chinese had taught Europeans something, and if they patronized the Hindu they would also teach them something. Those two peoples were highly civilised long before our forefathers were. There were only 150,000 Englishmen in India. One thousand years before Christ the Hindus were speaking one of the most beautiful languages—Sanskrit. If the Tallow peril came on they would have to depend upon their Hindu troops. Many a time a Hindu soldier had stood over en English lad to save him. The Hindus helped them in South Africa. India was now taking over Jesus Christ. They were coming from all the classes quickly. The Wesleyans had had missions in India for about a century, end they had got about one million converts. The Roman Catholics had been there since the time of the Reformation and they had converted about two millions. The nominal Christians in India at this moment were just 1 per cent, of the population. There I were about a hundred millions absolutely untouched by any missionary society, to whom the Gospel would be an absolutely new thing. It was hard for them to realise that. To reach India they would have to make use of every missionary in the world and give them enough work to do preaching to raw Hindus—so great was the problem. The Mohammedans in Indie were practically untouched. To meet the Mohammedans they had to be specially prepared for the work, us they had when they met the Unitarians and the Jews. The rev, gentleman gave instances of the hardships undergone by the missionaries. He had himself ridden miles in the 'scorching sun and then preached in the open market place. Ile had to teach the children to read ; for there were millions of children uneducated. The lower close people of India were treated by the higher class as if they belonged to another race. Every wet season cholera swept thousands away, and yet nobody cared. In the hot season they had smallpox and leprosy. There were hundreds of thousands of the victims of leprosy walking the streets of India. Over a million a year, over a period of ten years, had been swept away by plague His Gospel told him that God loved a black man as well as he loved him, and that was the message they were giving. Lord Morley had said in dealing with affairs in India, they should get into the skins of the people. They could only do that by the Gospel of Jesus Christ. Conclud- ing he said that he believed in Christianity as it was a historical religion. Christianity was the expression of the very highest. it was the human life and behaviour that drew him to it. He was glad to say that Chrie tian- ity was progressing by leaps and bounds in India. (Applause). Votes of thanks were accorded to the Chairman and the Rev. James Lewis.</p>
        <p>Readers who suffer with sudden sharp pains in the nerves and muscles of the body are advised to communicate with Dr. Williams' Afedieine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, stating their symptoms fully. All letters receive prompt replies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>AMBULANCE COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>AMBULANCE COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>;;;; ;</p>
        <p>The first competition of the ambulance teams belong- ing to the different works in the district was held at Earlestown Town Hall on Saturday last. Mr. A Smith presided, and was supported by Lord Newton (presi- dent), Messrs. C. B. F. Borron, A. C. Leslie, Blundell, T. Ormand, C. Emmett, W. Stone, and N. B. Halsall, Dr. Sutton, Dr. Valentine, and the Secretary (Mr. J. B. Porter), also several ladies. The winners were as follows :—McCorquadale's Works, challenge shield to be held for twelve months, the team receiving silver cruets ; second, Viaduct Waggon Works, handbags for the team : third, Collins Green Collery, umbrellas. Walter Duncan was the best all-round man, who won the individual merit prize of the Viaduct team, Earls- town.—Thanks were accorded Lord Newton, secretary and committee, and also to Dr. F. J. Knowles, for his impartial adjudication of the competing teams.</p>
