      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>" By Ovallst."</p>
        <p>Once more Warrington have realised expectations, —this time in losing at Wigan. It was not anticipated that they would survive the semi-final for the Lanca- shire Cup when it was known that they were drawn to meet the powerful Wigan team at Central Park. Under any circumstances the visiting team at Wigan enters upon the game with odds against. In this particular instance Wigan were absolutely at full strength, while Warrington were prevented from playing Treharne, Thomas, O'Neill, and Dowell by the qualification rule as to Lancashire Cup-ties. It seems rather absurd that stach old Warrington players ' as Thomas and Lees should be ineligible because they ' were not signed on by a certain date, and one can hardly acquit the Committee of all blame in the matter. Players are not always ready to sign at the opening of a season, but some special measures should be taken in order to prevent irritating difficulties of this kind. Under such conditions, even Fish might be ineligible on some future occasion. Imagine the rage of the crowd ! I hope the Committee which has displayed commendable activity this season—will take care that the signatures of F. Jenkins and F. 11. Shugars are , secured in time for the N.U. Cap.ties. They are men I who can ill be spared.</p>
        <p>In addition to the ineligibles, Brooks was also off the team on Saturday. The reasons seemed to be equally divided between an injured knee and the desire to be fit for the London test game to-day. Any- how Lloyd had to fill the vacancy, and while the team was weakened thereby, the sutstitute did not disgrace himself. As a matter of fact the team which Jr. Harry Ashton humorously described as "Warrington's auxiliary Cup team" gave a splendid display and came very near to defeating the Wiganers on their own patch. The Wirepullers fully maintained their reputation for Cup-tie prowess and were so little inferior to the Wigin men that the least bit of luck would have, at least, brought about a replay at Warrington. The final score was 15 points to 10 points in Wigan's favour, while ten minutes from the close Warrington were only a try behind and attack- ing strongly. Their supporters were delighted with the good show made by the " auxiliaries."</p>
        <p>The play is described in detail elsewhere, but it is necessary to say that if Warrington had taken all their opportunities—and they had more of them than Wiganthey would have won. For the first quarter of an hour they quite over-played the home team and Fish scored a brilliant try. Bradshaw had chances which should have brought a couple more, but he was weak at the critical moment, so that Wigan were only a try down by the time they had got their second wind. Then Leytham scored twice on Bradshaw's wing, and it says little for the Stockton Heath man's tackling powers that the Wigan crack finished the game as clean as he began. Bradsha'w lacks experi- ence of cup-tie football more than pluck, and he made the mistake of tackling high instead of low. A stronger man in his place might have brought about Wigan's downfall, for Leytham himself is weak when pressed.</p>
        <p>Though Warrington were ten points down soon after the interval they never lost heart. Dickenson gave Wigan five points by generously playing the ball with none of his own men up. Most players would have " foxed "for a moment or two and risked a penalty. However, Warrington soon reached the other end and Fish kicked a goal. Then a try followed to Jenkins, Fish again goaling, and the Wigan men quaked. A piece of foolish obstruction cost Warrington two more points, and Wigan breathed more freely with a margin of five, which they managed to keep to the end. On the attack they were a bit better than Warrington, but the presence of Brooks would have made all the difference. Warrington's defence was certainly finer than that of Wigan, who had most of what luck there was. Dickenson and Taylor were magnificent at centre, while Fish quite overplayed the much-praised Hiller. The forwards did yeoman service and Jenkins shone at outside half, where Lloyd gave him the ball cleanly and smartly. The Wigan star was Johnnie Thomas, who played nearly as well as he did at Warrington. The others wet their match in general play and tactics. The League table, up to date is as follows : For Agst. Per _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. eentage Wigan l3 12 1 0 57 65 309 17 15 79 9230 Oldham -.13 12 1 0 39 53 237 14 13 67 92.30 Halifax ll 10 1 0 25 47 191 8 14 58 9090 Hunslet -11 8 2 1 22 32 140 16 IS 86 77.27 WakefrldTl3 9 3 1 28 59 233 22 19 101 7307 Batley l3 9 3 1 21 30 132 19 20 98 73.07 Runcorn II 8 3 0 11 29 115 15 14 72 7272 Ebbw Vale 14 9 4 1 22 ;5. 179 23 28 133 6785 Keighley 13 8 1 1 38 46 214 14 28 112 65.38 Irddersll'ldls 8 4 1 24 40 168 26 27 133 60.00 Salford l3 T 6 0 30 25 135 22 21 116 5833 Merthyr Tl 2 6 5 1 18 27 117 31 21 134 5416 Hull 16 8 7 1 43 52 242 21 33 156 53.12 Leigh l2 7 5 0 23 25 121 19 15 83 5322 St.Helens 12 6 6 0 25 31 143 29 33 157 5000 Leeds l4 7 7 0 23 38 169 24 27 129 50.00 Dewsbury 12 6 6 0 25 26 128 23 25 121 50.00 Swinton -15 7 8 0 20 23 109 19 31 131 46.66 Hull K.R 12 5 6 1 35 35 175 29 30 148 45.83 Warrinen 12 5 6 1 25 21 122 18 23 105 4583 Broughtonl2 5 6 1 21 25 128 23 24 148 4583 Bradford 14 6 8 0 26 33 151 31 39 179 12.85 Barrow ll 4 7 0 17 16 82 21 35 153 3636 Treherbert 12 4 8 0 12 13 65 it 35 127 3333 Mid-Rh'da 11 3 8 0 10 16 68 25 32 146 2727 Widnes —ll 2 8 1 14 17 79 21 27 123 2272 York l3 210 1 23 23 115 34 51 230 19 23 Barry l3 211 0 7 14 56 39 72 294 15•38 Rochdaleli 12 111 0 7 12 50 29 33 157 8.33 Aberdare 12 111 0 16 22 98 44 62 274 8.33 Brumley l2 111 0 14 14 70 38 67 286 8'33</p>
        <p>Warrington and Wigan meet again to-day in the League, and the advantage of playing at home is once more with Wigan, Warrington had hopes of aveng- ing the cup-tie defeat but unfortunately the team available will be no stronger than last week. The only re-assuring feature is that Wigan are also handi- capped by the claims of the first test match with the Kangaroos in London. They supply Jenkins, Thomas and Silcock, while Warrington send Dickenson and Brooks. O'Neill makes a third absentee, his suspen- sion for the Runcorn incident covering to-day's match'. The teams are thus on more of au equality than was the case last week, but Wigan have stronger reserves. Warrington will not enter on the contest without some hope of success, however. If they can pick up a little 'of the luck which was denied them a week ago they may create a surprise.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team will be as follows :—Tilley; Fish, Taylor, Cartwright and Treharne • Lloyd and Jenkins ; Thomas, Dowell, A.Boardman, 'P.l3oardman, Chesters and Hewitt. The first reserve is Jordan, whom I would rather have seen selected before Cartwright.</p>
        <p>Wigan will turn out with Gunn ; Leytham,Sharrock, Todd, Miller ; Glee, Ned Jones ; Ramsdale, Barton, Whittaker, Blears, Johnston, and Cheatham!</p>
        <p>Almost as much interest will be centred in the "A" team contest at Wilderspool, for the struggle between the two teams for the Combination Championship is as keen as ever. It will be remembered that Warring- ton created a surprise by beating the Wiganers on their own ground early in the season, and they should repeat the performance to-day. Their feat last Satur- day was one which merits special praise, for they con- quered what was practically the St. Helens first team at Knowsley-road by 3 points to nil.</p>
        <p>The "A" team will be: Mclntyre, full back ; Landor, Jordan, Oakes, and Bradshaw, three-quarters: Lees and Daintith, half backs; Belton, Harmer, harrison, Gibbs, Bradshaw and Forster, forwards.</p>
        <p>The first test match takes place to-day on the ground of the Queen's Park Rangers Club, Park Royal, London. The chosen thirteen are: H. Gifford (Barrow), back; W. Batten (ifunslet), B. Jenkins (Wigan), G. F. Dickinson (Warrington), G. Tyson (Oldham), three-quarters); E. Brooks (Warrington), and J. Thomas (Wigan), hall-backs; W. Jukes , (flunslet), A. Mann (13f-adford), A. Robinson (Halifax), W. Longworth (Oldham), J. laigaon (Elunslet and A. Smith (Oldham), forwards.</p>
        <p>Hockey. Clayton First failed to turn up last Saturday at Latchford.</p>
        <p>Latchford Second, however, had the pleasure of encountering them at Clayton, and in a very close game , were defeated 24. Minaros scored a couple of clever</p>
        <p>goals for the visitors, but the second was disallowed, though nobody know why.</p>
        <p>To-day the First Eleven meet Waterloo at Waterloo. The team is :—W. Unsworth, goal ; C. Plinston, T. Dixon, backs; F. Newell, J. B. Wilkinson, J. Albinson, halves ; D. Travis, H. Parker Scott, E. Banks, W. Spensley, forwards. Referee, Mr. W. B. Cobb. Train leaves Central 1.50.</p>
        <p>The Second team at home against Manchester Y.M.C.A. is :—J. Appleton, goal; J. Kenworthy, W. H. Pickles, backs ; A. Newton, A. Knight, F. Tole, halves ; J. Wilkinson, A. Starkey, R. Jones, W. H. Minards, C. Hackney, forwards. Referee, Mr. H. Warburton. Bully-off 2.43.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Sciatica's Swift Pains rendered this Lady helpless.</head>
        <p>Sciatica's Swift Pains rendered this Lady helpless.</p>
        <p>tier case had defied treatment, but Dr. Williams' Pink Pills succeeded by curing the cause of Sciatica.</p>
        <p>Considering that one touch of Sciatica makes the whole system quiver with pain, the reader will readily recognize how terrible must have been the ordeal of Mrs. Cole, of West Ham, who for many months was afflicted with Sciatica so acutely that the best skill seemed powerless to help her. When describing, at her home, 40, Mircus-street, West Ham, Essex, the torments that seized her before she turned to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills and obtained a welcome cure, Mrs. Cole said :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>STONE COLD AND BLOODLESS.</head>
        <p>STONE COLD AND BLOODLESS.</p>
        <p>"For weeks I felt worn oat every day, and at nights slept badly. MY food did not agree with me. I became very bloodless, my hands and feet went as cold as stone, and my strength dwindled." "I could not get about without great pain and difficulty ; for instance, when crossing the room I had to hobble from chair to chair. Such exertion left me very breathless, and my heart would palpitate against my ribs. I looked like a skeleton ia a skin. Then I became very nervous.</p>
        <p>ARROWS OF AW)I,ZY</p>
        <p>" Soon every time I attempted •to use my arms cutting pains shot across my shoulders and down my back. The muscles of my loin felt sore and twisted and when I bent my back an agony like sharp hot arrow, went flying throng: me, even down to my ankles. One da. such a fierce pain seized me that I felt helpless on the float and had to be carrie,_ to bed, where I lay a helpless Sciatic cripple. As I lay in bed my head was</p>
        <p>RACKED WITII VIOLENT NEURALGIA and to make matters worse the pains settled in my joints particularly in my toes. " Doctors gave ma different medicines for Sciatica, and I was rubbed daily with embrocations and ironed with hot irons until my flesh became too sore to be touched. I was sinking, when my husband said, 'I can't make out why you don't take the pills that cured our boy Charlie. Then I began to take Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. "After I had taken a few boxes of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills I enjoyed refreshing sleep at nights, and my hand and feet felt comfortably warm. I persevered with these pills and was soon able to get about again. My veins began to fill with rich blood ; my nerves became strong and steady. I had a better appetite, and regained my lost flesh. Steadily the p tins of Sciatica left me, and I have never felt anything of Sciatica or Weakness since." THE DR. WILLIAMS' WAY</p>
        <p>Sciatica is one of the worst forms of Neuralgia, and Mrs. Cole's cure is another proof that Nervous Dis- orders can be cured only by making the Blood Rich and Red, and so nourishing the nerves. This is just what Dr. Williams' Pink Pills accomplish—they make Good Blood. Thus they have repeatedly cured Neuralgia, Rheumatism, St. Vitus' Dance, Nervous Breakdown, and the aches and ills of women. 2s. 9d. for one box, or 13s. 9d. for six, post free, from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London Sold also by dealers, but insist upon having only the genuine Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. These seven words are on the wrapper of every genuine box.</p>
        <p>Alety • '4:Qt.. • ' is / LY n it ,441r,_ lr d " I fell heltless the floor."</p>
