      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>RETIREMENT OF POLICE INSPECTOR</head>
        <p>RETIREMENT OF POLICE INSPECTOR</p>
        <p>SMITH.</p>
        <p>A Long and Active Constabulary Career.</p>
        <p>Inspector Samuel Smith, who retired from the Borough Force, on pension, on November 26th has a record of service of 26 years in Warrington and nearly five years in Cheshire. Although a native of Shrop- shire the ex-inspector's youth was spent in the colliery district of Hope, Flintsbire, whore, at an early age, he went to work in the mines. On attaining manhood he decided to go in for the constabulary, and obtained an appointment in the Cheshire County Police, in Novem- ber, 1877. After the usual sojourn at headquarters he was drafted for duty to New Brighton, then, it need hardly be said, a very different place from what it is now. Mr. Smith has many interesting recollections of I' the New Brighton of that period, when the Police staff consisted of a sergeant and one constable, with lan extra P.C. for duty on the sands in the summer season. Leaving New Brighton he was transferred to ' Runcorn. and was afterwards stationed successively at , Preston Brook and Grappenhall. Whilst at the latter 11 place he was in close touch with Warrington and, at the suggestion of a local magistrate, he decided to throw in his lot with the Borough establishment. He applied to the then Chief Constable, Mr. Hunt, was accepted, and joined the Borough Police in November, ; 1832, quitting the Cheshire Constabulary with a first- class discharge. The Borough Force at that time con- sisted of only 32 men, and, the local guardians of the peace had a much rougher time of it than they have now. Nine years after joining Mr. Smith was pro- moted Sergant, and on the retirement of the late Mr. Rowbotham, in 1905, he was appointed Inspector. It may truly be said of Mr. Smith's long period of 31 years' constabulary service that it was a record of useful work, energetically and faithfully performed. The ex-Inspector all his life was an indefatigable worker, and as an officer he neither spared himself nor excused his subordinates. Of a quiet disposition, but possessed of a strong will, and fearless and straightforward in all his dealings, he neither sought for,nor valued the favour or good opinion of those with whom he came in contact. Though a strict disciplinarian, Inspector Smith entered with zeal into anything which was caculated to be for the benefit of the members of the force, and he carried with him into his recreations the same untiring l energy that characterized him in his every-day work. In this connection it may be mentioned that he was one of the most active members of the Police Athletio I Club ever since its inauguration. For some time he acted as assistant secretary to the organization, and during his office as such the club reached its high-water mark of prosperity, largely as a result of Mr. Smith's ' " spade work." Again, the financial success of the Police Annual Sports was due in no small measure to Inspector Smith, who always headed the list in the sale of tickets. Mr. Smith's chief recreation,however, was cycling, a pastime to which he was devotedly attached. Much of his annual leave of absence from duty was spent a-wheel, and he never missed an opportunity of indulging in a long spin through the surrounding country. A few years ago he cycled from Warrington to John 0' Groats, and in the following year from Warrington to Land's End. He has also toured exten- sively in Lancashire, Cheshire and North Walerl. We understand that a movement is on foot in the Police Force to present Mr. Smith with a suitable testimonial on his retirement. Whilst he was esteemed by all his comrades, he specially earned the confidence and regard of the younger members of the force, who were much attached to him.</p>
        <p>A Refreshing Drink and a Sustaining Food.</p>
      </div>
