      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay.</head>
        <p>Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay.</p>
        <p>At this mission on Sunday the services were con- ducted by the Rev. H. H. Zizijian, a native preacher from Armenia. At the evening service he spoke on the Armenian massacres and portrayed the terrible suffer- ings of the Christians of that country. There were 70,000 widows and orphans practically destitute, and, though the signs of the times were very favourable, many years would have to pass before that blot in history would be wiped out. Miss Yazijian, the missioner's daughter, sang and recited in the English and Armenian languages. In the schoolroom a lantern service was held and the room was crowded with chil- dren. " The elrly history of Christian people " was the subject dealt with by Captain G. R. Crosfleld.</p>
        <p>Early Latchford Methodism. Alderman Ellison gave some interesting reminiscences of Early Latchford Methodism in the course of an address to the Men a Bible Class at the Latchford Wesleyan Church on Sunday. He took his hearers back to the year 1810,when the Primitives had a"cause" in the Old-road. Eventually the Wdsleyans started in a room over a shop in Wash-lane, thence removing to the " Old Barn " at the top of Thelwall-lano. Realising the necessity of a more commodious structure, the Temper- ance Hall was rented from the Good Templar body, and ultimately, in 1893, the present church was opened by. the Bev. Walford Green, President of the Conference Methodism, said Alderman Ellison, had had its way to make at Latchford, and to-day they were reaping what those of another day had sown. He hoped his hearers would be urged on to greater effort by the example of the past. Mr. Ellison was thanked for his interesting address, on the proposition of Mr. Atkin, seconded by Mr. Ford, and supported by Messrs. Stone and Ronald. Confirmation Service at St. Paul's Church. The Bishop of Liverpool (Dr. Chavasse) administered the rite of confirmation to 173 candidates at St. Paul's Church on Monday evening, in the presence of a large congregation. The candidates came from the follow- ing parishes --St. Paul's, 32 males and 69 females ; S. Peter's, 7 males and 17 females ; St. 13arnabas', 27 males and 21 females. The Bishop delivered two impressive addresses to the candidates before and after the laying on of hands, and pointed out that there were two gates through which every member of the Church of England was expected to pass—Baptism by which they entered Christ's visible church and Call-. flrmation which was the gateway to the Holy Table. His Lordship mentioned the interesting fact that 46 years had elapsed since he was confirmed, and yet the confirmation day, the place and the bishop's address were still vividly with him. The clergy present were the Rays. W. Braceeamp, E. E. Peters, J. H. Banlces, J. H. G. Hollingworth, J. W. Glendenning, B. Percival and J. H. Garnett.</p>
        <p>Religious Education. With the approval of the Bishop of Liverpool. Sunday last was observed as "Education Sunday " at the various churches in the diocese. Special sermons were preached at the churches of Warrington, in which opportunity was taken to plead the cause of religious education. Preaching at St. Barnabas' Church in the evening, the Rev. J. Bell Cox, Vicar of St. Margaret's, Princes-road, Liverpool, took for his text the words "Thanks be to God for Ws unspeakable gift" (II Cor. ix., 15). He emphasised the great value of intercession and said he believed the abandonment of the Govern- ment Education Bill was an answer to prayer. It was impossible for members of the Church to accept pro- posals from any Government which provided that religious instruction in the schools should be deter- mined by and given under the authority of educational committees and local authorities composed of men of all creeds, or no creed at all. It was equally impossible for Churchmen to accept proposals to impose on the country a system of undenorninational religion which by what it excluded was necessarily hostile to that denominational religion which the Church, and its schools were built to main- tain. Such proposals must be resisted to the very utmost in and out of Parliament. They asked for no favour for themselves and for nothing they would not willingly grant to others. They asked that their education should not be divorced from religion, and that the State should not establish and pay for a mutilated Christianity. They claimed that fair play and equal dealing should be dealt out to all alike. They claimed in the name of justice that no preference should be given to undenominational religion ; that religious instruction should b 3 given in the school in school hours by those in regard to whom there was security that they believed what they taught. They claimed that the Bible should not be treated RS a mere reading book or a convenient vehicle for ethical instruc- tion. They belitved that there could be no real educa- tion apart from faith in the person of our incarnate Lord Jesus Christ, both God and man. Other special preachers were :—St. Paul's Church,</p>
        <p>thellev. E. S. Wymott, vicar of St. Bridget's, Waver. tree ; St. Peter's Church, the Rev. W. A. Wickham vicar of St. Andrew's, Wigan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WINWICIC ASYLUM.</head>
        <p>WINWICIC ASYLUM.</p>
        <p>£20,090 Extension Opened.</p>
        <p>The new annexe at Winwick Asylum for the accom- modation of 120 idiot children and 12 female adult patients was formally opened by the Chairman of the Winvvick Asylum Committee, Mr. S. S. Brown, on Thursday afternoon. The Chairman of the Lancashire Asylums' Board, Mr. N.W. Helme, M.P., the Winwick Asylum Committee, and other members of the Board who are members of the Committees of the other Lancashire Asylums, were also present. After an inspection of the buildings the company met in one of the day-rooms,and Mr. Brown on behalt of the Witiwick Committee, declared the building open. Mr. Brown' said that when the Asylums' Board bought the estate in 1893 there was a large building already on the estate which had been the rectory and it was altered for the accommodation of 50 idiot children. That building was first occupied by the children in September, 1897, and being an old one had rapidly deteriorated to such an extent that it had been found necessary to erect a building to take its place. This Committee therefore applied to the Lancashire Asylums' Board for money for that purpose' and the Board after considering the questior came to the conclusion that it irould be a wise thing to erect a new building, which would not only be large enough to take the present inmates, but also to accommodate a much larger number, viz., 120 children, and 12 adult female patients who would help in the domestic work of the place. IVith such a large number of patients in a detached building, it was necessary also to provide accommodation for a Medical Officer, as well as for the matron and staff. Provision had also been made for a teacher, so that those who were capable of receiving instruction would be taught. The Asylums' Board voted the Committee £21,000 for the erection of this new building, which with an additional £990 found necessary to cover the cost of certain additions and impiovements of the original scheme, covered the whole cost of the building which was now not only complete. but paid for, with the exception of the b dance of the retention money which worid be paid to the contractors in due course. The r )st per bed for the 132 beds thus worked out to the veiy low figure of a little over £l6O. He thought that having had an opportunity of inspecting the building they would agree with him that the Com- mittee had successfully endeavoured to combine efficiency with economy, in which endeavour they had been well supported by their architect (Mr. Thomas Chadwick). They could say that the building was a substantial one, suitable in every way for its purpose, and, he thought he could safely say, would serve that purpose'fbr many years to come. He hoped the Asylums' Board would now consider the question of erecting a similar building for the accommodation of girls. With regard to the main building which some of them had that day seen for the 11 t time, he would like to say that the total cost of it wh.ch included the Land Railway Siding and Asylum Buildings, Cottages,Lodges and Farms, was £460,000; thee 3corumodation being for 2,000 patients, the cost per bed worked out at the very low figure of £230. If they would compare this with the cost of the recently built London and Yorkshire Asylums, which cost from £4OO to £430 per bed, they would see that the main building bad been erected with tho same strict regard to economy. Mr. Ileline in proposing a vo,e of thanks to Mr. Brown for declaring the buileing open, said he was glad that Mr. Brown had that day received a further mark of the confidence of his committee who had re-elected him as thei- chairmrn. He was glad to think that he was a member of the Asylums' Board which bad done, and is doing, so much for the mentally afflicted of this County. In the report of the Royal Commission on the feeble-minded, wuich was now before Parliament, they had a careful survey of the whole of the question, and it was satifactory to think that die Lancashire Asylums Board, and in particular this C3ta- ruittee were taking the lead in this pa,:tic.,lllr bi.inch of work, i.e., the care and treatment of feeble- minded children. This building which he had had the pleasure of inspecting ap,mred to him in every way well adapted for its purpose. He endorsed the hope expressed by Mr. Brown that the Board woald take into consideration the question of providing siaa:lar accommodation for girls. Dr. Rhodes seconded. He had also inspected the build- ing and fully endorsed the remarks of both Mr. Brown and Mr. llelme as to its suitability for treating these children. He thought the Committee had got the best possible building for the purpose. In his capacity as a Visiting Justice at Strangeways Gaol he had observed that a large proportion of the inmates of the prison were feeble-minded, which emphasized the need of such a building as this for the care and treatment of this 'class Stan early age, and he hoped that the other counties of England would follow the lead which Lancashire had given them in dealing with this problem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>FATAL FALL.—An inquest was held at Stockton Heath on Monday afternoon by Mr. T. Ridgway, on the body of Frances Broder, of 2, Bedford-street, who died on Saturday night from injuries inflicted by a fall downstair —The deceased, who was the widcw of Mr. Charles Oswald Broder, a former Californian rancher, had resided for about four years with Mr. and Mrs. Richtual Heyes. She was 92 years of age. She was going upstairs at 8.30 on Saturday night when she fell to the bottom. She was picked up and placed one sofa, rad Dr. Howard sent for. She was unconscious and died shortly afterwards.—The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
