        <p>has the millennium on hand roady for delivery, were it not too easily apparent that Mr. Rylands is really only a humblq sparrow masquerading as a silver-voiced canary. The dishonest hawker occasionally retails a bird of this description, but its bright plumage vanishes under the eflacts of the first shower or an early rub from adversity. Clearly the new Conservatism, though so bright and entrancing, won't wash I</p>
        <p>We cannot call Warrington WARRINGTON Bridge a hsrdy annual BRIDGE. exactly, because it is really a hoary centurion, but the question of replacing it with a new struc- ture seems likely to occupy our municipal administrators for the rest of their natural lives—unless the present MAyor proves m)re pertinacious and fortunate than his pre- decessors. Alderman Forshaw took a sort of knightly oath on succeeding to the Mayoral chair that he would get the the new Warrington Bridge scheme through. He is, at least, to be complimented on not allowing the grass to"•grow on the buttressea of the old ITridg for the matter was before the General Purposes Committee 1, in tangible form this weak. A resolution to proceed with a new iron single-span ! bridge was actually carried, but we are not consp;cuously elated by this circumstance. Both the General Purposes Committee and the C ouncil have come to definite decisions in the p ist wth regard to Warrington Bridge. Lls a threatened man, however,it lives long and may quite possibly outlive the latest proposal. There is a vast difference between passing these resolutions and carrying them into effect, as both the Council and the public have learnt to their cost. All manner of cbfliculties at once crop up and already there is a split in the camp with ragard b the width of the new Bridge. The majority favour a generous scheme of 80 feet, but a very determined minority insist that 60 feet is enough, and seeing that Bridge-street only measures 66 feat from wall to ! wall, the latter provision would seem to be ample. ' The larger scheme may involve the disturbance e of licensed premises at an altogether dispropor- tionate cost, and the question is one which must not be settled rashly. Toe greatest inducement ! for the Council to posh on wth the scheme is the present low price of iron and steel, on the purchase ! of which about £2,000 might be saved as compared with the prices ruling eighteen months ago. We hope the members of the Council will be able to settle their differences in time to " c itch the . market."</p>
        <p>There can be little doubt PEERS AND in the public mind to-day PEOPLE. that this is taci!•, after all, a democratically governed country inhabited by a free people. Neither is it a monarchy or a republic. Onservers abroad are accustomed to look upon England as an example of government by the people for the people, based upon trust in the people. Li theory it is all this, but in actual practice our boasted freedom of government is something far diffetont. Of late years it has become increasingly apparent that we have just so much government by and for the people as the House of hereditary peers is disposed to allow. Reforms may be demanded, and their necessity emphas'zad by the elected Commons of the arm itry, and in the end be summarily squashed by the H mse of Lords, whose power of veto is exercised with a partizan arbitrariness which cannot he calmly tolerated. The treatment of that great measure, the Licensing Bill, is fresh in the public memory, and we hope no opportunity will be lost of emphasizing the deep resentment felt in all directions by the enlightened and progressive majority of the country's inabitants. To-night great meeting of protest against the Lords and their arrogant assumption of autocratic rule will be held in Parr Hall. We hope all Warringtonians who love fair play and recognise the need for reforms in the British Constitution will gather together and express themselves with an emphasis which cannot be mistaken or ignored. Writing prior to the Premier's groat speech of last night, the " Westminster Gazette " discusses the general issue upon which the Liberal Party will have to fight the next General Election. " says this authoritative journal" the Liberal Party, though in power, with an immense majority, cannot make its views prevail on Eduction or Licensing, it cannot make them prevail on any controversial issue. Even if it came back to power with a big maj irity at the end of another election, it would, as things are, have no security that its policy would not be wrecked even in the first Session of Parliament, as it was in 19)6. We need a spokesruan who will disengage this question from any incidental issue, whether Licensing, E lucation, Piatal Voting, Scottish Land, and present it to the country as the big constitutional lean° that it is, involving the whole system of Government and the fate of the party system. If the Prime Minister will rally the country on this issue, we believe he will find that there is a great reserve of opinion which though it may be divided on particular questions, will be united on this. Toe real question is not that of the theoretical rights of the House of Lords, hut of the best method of conducting this campaign and it cannot, in our opinion be 'conducted effectively if the public gets it into its head that the Liberal Party is inclined to shirk the issue and afraid to risk its life in maintaining its po:ition. If the life of the Government is to bo prolonged, it must be a fighting life, in whic't all the resources beyond , the reach of the House of Lords are used boldly and effectively up to the point when they are exhausted, and at that point the question must be referred to the country. L3t us be under no mistake that the present party system must pass out of existence if there is to be no limit te the power of one party to pies any leeisletion in ay., teeth of any popelar movement fir the space of six years, while the other party is to b 3 :ar, the mercy of its opponents from the first hour of its existence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>....,...m.....e• NATIONAL SIKVIGE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>....,...m.....e• NATIONAL SIKVIGE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>A meeting to further the objects of this League was held in the Christ Church Schoolroom, Latchford, on Thursday evening. The Rev. O. C. Briggs, presided over a fair attendance, and in opening the proceedings said he thought the objects of the League were in every way excellent and worthy of support. Mr. Townroe opened his lecture by inviting those present to visit Orford Barracks during drilling time, when they would probably hear an ofter bawling at his men for being stupid. He would give utterance to. a phrase which was very common in military quarters: " Come out and look at yourself." (Laughter.) That was what he (the lecturer)wanted the people to do—"to come out and look at themselves." It was the duty of every man to help in the defence of his country, and to be trained for that purpose and to he able to handle a rifle. Mr. Townroe detailed the objects of the League and contended that it was not prudent to ' depend entirely upon the navy to protect us. Three years ago our spies discovered a scheme of a foreign power to invade England. They were going to land at Hull, and march across the country to Liverpool. Their plan was to invade the manufacturing districts and so break the backbone of the country. The lecturer drew attention to the Dogger Bank outrage, when a Russian warship fired upon our fishing smacks. Where was our home fleet then? Most of the vessels were cruising in the English Channel, and six were in dry dock at the time. It must be borne in mind that thousands of the youths were physically unfit for military or naval training Out of the 50,000 volunteers 1 for service in the navy last year 30,000 were considered unfit. From the moral and physical points of view the National Service League proposals would be of great benefit to the country. Youths from the towns'who went for three months training under canvas would be greatly benefited by the fresh air and wholesome food. The scheme would do something to arrest the physical deterioration of the country and there would be au improvement of character. (Applause.) Mr. Harvey also addressed the meeting and said the people who were always ready to make use of catch phrases, such as "Bull Dogs,' and "we will pull through same as last time," were the first to • funk in time of need. Tree, we had pulled through in days gone by but unless we were prepared the next attack might prove the downfall of the nation. It would be a good thing If youths instead of smoking and drinking would join the National Service League and go for three months' military training. The country could not do without physical training any longer. (Applause.) Various questions were put, and answered, Mr. Townroe stating that it was intended that full provision should be made foridependents while the breadwinner was away in camp,and that employers should arrange to keep situations open until the men's return. Votes of thanks were accorded to the speakers on the proposition of Mr. Firth (Police Court Missionary), seconded by Captain Waring and supported by Mr. F. Cobb.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>There is most attractive fare this week for the patrons of this popular house of entertainment. Every turn reaches a high standard of excellence and public appreciation is accorded in generous measure. Von Etta heads the bill and introduces one of the most remarkable shows ever presented on the music-hall stage. The lady is handsome and striking, and one feature of her performance is to appear in 24 different costumes, many of which are assumed and discarded in the interval of a couple of seconds. A busier act altogether we have not seen. Von Etta performs some sensational feats of illusion, in which she and her helpers disappear myst,erously after being apparently boxed up securely. As a sleight of hand expert she is most proficient, and there are elec- trical effects which are used to full advan- tage when Von Etta dances. Something is going on all the time and it is a crowded period of 20 minutes while Von Etta holds the stage. There was only one thing which jarred from our point of view, and that was the old-fashioned trick of firing a revolver when the sensational denouement of some trick was expected. An audience has nerves if the artiste has not, and Von Etta would do well to save , her cartridges. Ventriloquists have been a popular , feature of the Hippodrome bill just lately and Alpho with his doll as "Tommy the Farmer's Boy" is quite up to the standard of his predecessors. Lecardo Bros distinguish themselves as comedy acrobats and are unquestionably a smatV pair. Tom Yorke appears with success in the rold of a Cockney character comedian, A couple of songs with choruses appealing strongly to the "gods." There is originality and cleverness in the turn given by the Melbournes. Their comedy "duo" is one of the brightest and most laughable seen here since Tom and Marie Motramo convulsed the audiences. The Melbournes are by no means slavish imitators and deserve the hearty applause which falls to their lot. A unique item is that provided by Taree, the Australian bushman who treats the audience to some of the music Nshiel; one might find at the camp-fire in the bush, and be agreeably surprised. Taree is a genius with a five shilling harp and a mouth organ. He plays the two instruments simultaneously, and manipulates the month organ in astonishing fashion solely by the 1 movement of his tongue and lips. Taree's repertoire of music is extensive and peculiar, and his skill brings down the house. The Milton trio combine singing, dancing and paper pattern tearing in a curious medley of sound and movement. Their efforts were cordially appreciated. The orchestra and bio-tableaux bring up the rear in satisfactory style. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Another capital programme is announced for next week. The bill is headed by W. J. Churchill (Uncle Willie), the popular comedian, and other attractions will be :—Astley's American Circus, described as the funniest act set before the public and introducing the great riding machine; The Donatos in their clever acrobatic act ; Venturini, the celebrated Continental illusion inventor, who will present the latest and greatest illusion entitled "The Mysterious Bedroom " ; Johnny Holland and Co. in their successful absurdity "The Boat Builder " ; Margaret Radcliffe, illustrated songsters; Marion Armitage, the comedy girl.</p>
        <p>For Influenza and Bronchial Conghs,Woode Great Peppermint Cure. ls. lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
        <p>When Mr. BIitRELL intro- MR. BIRRELL'S ducecl his Irish L trid Bill REVELATIONS. to the House of Commons</p>
        <p>he held members:breathless by his account of the costly blunders made:by Mr. GEORGE WYNDHAM—blunders that have now become " gross as a mountain, open, palpable." Liberals may fairly and without ostentation claim to have perceived in advance some of the worst faults of Mr. WYNDHAm's measure. They denounced its extravagance. They condemned the large bribe offered to the landlowners. They said that burdens would be entailed on the Fisch isers of Irish land which might be repudiated on the groued that the Government of that day was making a bargain for the landlords as against the tenants. Mr. BIRRELL'S speech in introducing his Bill was more than a justification for these predictions. Mr. WYNDHAM'S estimates of what the operation under his measure would cost have been almost doubled. We have now to speak of £180,000,000, instead of £100,000,000. We have now to take into account a greatly-increased bonus to the landlords. We are called upon to contemplate a considerable grant from the Exchequer as a con dition under which further land purchase can be carried on with any measure of justice to the purchaser. And all because Mr. Wyrrnnext acted under landlord pressure, without quite knowing what sort of problemf,he was dealing with. It has always been granted:ithat:Mr. WHAM is a Statesman whose intentions are admirable. Unfortunately he is not quite a business man, and his sympathies are with'his class. Thera can be no doubt that he was greatly imposed on when he brought forward the unlucky piece'of legisla- tion which Mr. BIRRELL is at present endeavour. jug to mend. We cannot expect him to admit this, of course ; but there is a;sort of implied confession in the speech which he madelon the second reading of the now Irish.:Land Bill. He did not attempt to deal with the main points of Mr. BIRRELL'S amazing speech, the reason given being that in the fortnight which has elapsed from the introduc- tion of the Bill he has not had time to study its provisions.. This strikes us as ft very poor ground to ask for its rejection, which is what 31r. WYNDHAM asked for on Monday. The late Chief ISecretary says that he had not tom able to give to Mr. BIRRELL'S proposals that close considers- Lion which, as he admits, they demand; yet he asks unhesitatingly for their rejection, and enters on a rambling critieism—brilliant in itself, no doubt, but treatiug of almost everything but the situation with which it is essential ts deal.</p>
        <p>It may be said at once that such criticisms do not matter,except in so far as they are a prophecy of what will happen in the House of Lords. That branch of Parliament rests under an enormous responsibility in c)nnection with the Irish question with Irish discontent, with the partial depopula- tion of Ireland. All tlae:good it could possibly do could never balance the evil it has accomplished. Yet here is another measure on which it is likely to lay its evil hand, a measure which is a real message:of peace, and considers the case of the , landlord no less than that of the tenant. If Mr. WYNDHAM had thought of it, be might have pro- duced just such a B ll as than of Mr. Bflumt. But as the Bill comes from a Liberal Government, it is to meet with uncontrolled hostility, with results such as have been produced before when measures of conciliation designed by wise Statesmanship havo been trampled under foot, in what were supposed to be the interests of laud lordism. Mr. BIRRELL'S Bill has nothing to fear from the House of Commons, and certainly not under the inspiration of such speeches as that of Mr. WYNDHAM. Bit this is another case in which whatever is done in the one House can be much more speedily undone in the other. And so it is that we h ive the Irish land question with us always.</p>
        <p>- We notice with surprise MR. RYLANDS AND that there is a strain— THE NEW perverted 'tis true—but CONSERVATISNI. Still a strain of Liberalism in the blood of Mr. W. P. Rylands. Speak;ng at the ,Thuior Conservative Club last week h 3 endeavotired to mAke it clear that it was not he who had changed, but Conservatism. This is what ha is reported to have said :</p>
        <p>"He held that Toryism as it used to be wader- ' stood was as dead as Queen Anne—(laughter)—and in his opinion Conservatism was a force which was based on the principles which were recognized in the old days as the true Liberal principles. They were endeavouring to claim as the basis of their policy 'Peace, retrenchment, reform, freedom, and equality.' These were the foundations of Con- servatism to-day." Even Mr. Ryland has never made a more r ,markable declaration, and we wonder if it will be subscribed to by other official Conservatives He admits that Toryism is "as dead as Queen Anne,"—which we do not challenge— but we are certainly astonished to read that the new Conservatism is " based on the principles whic't were recognised in the old days as the trio Liber-il principles," 113 even specified the basis of their policy as " peace. retrenchment, reform, freedom and equality." As Liberals we ought to feel flattered rather than aggrieved at this absorption hulas bolus by Con- servatism of the whole bssis of Liberalism. Mr. Rylands has written so much about Socialibm that he has evidently converted one unbeliever—him- self. "Freedom and equality " have surely been borrowed from Blatchfurd and Grayson. The next thing to anticipate is that the Conservative party</p>
      </div>
