        <head>POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>It is to be hoped the latest Warrington Bridge ! scheme will materialise. We know one ratepayer who ' [FROM 01:711. LONDON CORIMSPONDENT.] has been uttering awful anathemas this week because The withdrawal of the Government Education Bill he was nearly scraped off the footpath across the on Monday was a bitter disappointment to all those bridge by a passing lorry load of " bonds " from who had earnestly hoped for a permanent settlement one of the tanneries. of the controversies which have done S 3 much harm to . . the cause of education. But no other course was possible. The only condition under which the Government would have been justified in persevering with the measure had not been fulfilled. Still, as Mr. Asquith said in a speech which by its sincerity made a deep impression upon the House, " the intro- duction of the Bill will never be regretted or repented of, by any of those who had had any share in it, for the sake of what it has accomplished in bringing into organized existence and into articulate expres- sion the vast body of opinion from tnen of all sections, creeds, and parties in this county in favour of con- ciliation and peace." The time which would have been required for the Education Bill is now left free for other business, but even s, Parliament has a very arduous spell of work before it from now to the end of the session.</p>
        <p>* , *</p>
        <p>Those parents who are particularly anxious_ to find something novel and striking in the way of Christmas presents for their children should take note of the fact that a skull, a maypole, aa elephant's foot, and two artificial legs have just been included in an auction of goods left in railway-carriages. * * * The Ilayor of Warrington is as proud of his youth- ful associations with the National schools as he is of the fact that he is also an" Old Blue," and it waa a fitting and graceful act that he should mark his eleva- tion to the Borough's highest office by visiting the schools and addressing a few words of excellent advice to the boys. His Worship had a great recep- tion and the scholars' regard for him was still more enhanced when he begged them a hal holiday.</p>
        <p>* * * THE FATAL ROCK. Having seen the suffragettes foiled at Warrington, l It is of prime importance to have a clear and dis- Mr. Birrell is evidently not disposed to be bothered tinct view of the rock upon which negotiations were by them again if he can help it. Last night he was finally careened, for a great deaf of misrepresentation speaking at Nottingham, when only about 20 tickets has been imported into the case. Mr. Balfour throws were issued to ladies who were personally known to all the blame upon the maladroitness of the Govern- the Liberal agent. They. had to enter by a special ment, in postponing to the last those details of finance door an hour before the time of the meeting, and be ; which have assumed a fatal significance. It is a personally passed by the agent. Some of the fair plauaible contention but there is no substance in it. ladies of Nottingham probably feel annoyed with It is altogether misleading to say, as Mr. Balfour has their more militant sisters. I said, that what the Church was to give up was put into * * * I black and white, and all that it was to receive was On Wednesday the Amalgamated Society of Rail- , left vague. The right of entry into Council as well as way Servants decided to enter an appeal against the transferred schools was a great , great in the finding of the Appeal Court in regard to levies upon ' sacrifice of Nonconformist scruples, which it involved members of trade unions for the support of repre- • I and great m the opportunities which it afforded to sentatives in Parliament. The House of Lords will ' the Church. The Bishop of Southwark has described now be asked for its decision. The society has taken, ; the sacrifices made by Nonconformists as "enormous." we think, the right course. It is of the first import- ; Even Lord Hugh Cecil acknowledged the right of ance that the law should be settled beyond all dispute, ' entry to be a" a valuable thing." In the details of and that cannot be said to be the case u