        <p>* * The Panama Canal has so long stool as a symbol of vast failure that it is a little difficult for the ordinary man to attune himself to the reception of the facts which Dr. Vaughan Cornish recently put before the Royal Geographical Society. He has made a recent survey of the Canal, and he calculates that at the present rate of working the Canal will be open for traffic in another seven years. The undertaking is one of an enormously costly character, but it is moving, and when it is onnpleted it will take the largest ironclad afloat, and will reduce all the distances from this country to the West Coast of America by an average of many thousands of miles. American enterprise seems capable of carrying through the work which brought such disaster to all engaged in its early stairs. * It is the half-foolish and the half-wise who are the most dangerous.—Gocatc. Good dinners are so much rarer than good women; and far more piquant. —G. B Burgin. Selfishness is a detestable vice which every man is conscious of in himself but can never forgive in others. Pascal. A good name is a second life.—Longfellow. Kings and authors should be very careful how they treat their subjects. —Hart. In the game of life men moqt frequently play the knave and women the deuce. —ficnripc. Gloomy theology is more to ha condemned than scepticism, for while this only doubts the true the other affirms the false.—Trhittnore.</p>
        <p>, through the pooling of grants, to apply its funds more effectively; and it would receive the boon for which it scarcely dared to hope —of entry into the Council schools. When, in addition to this, Mr. Runciman consented to double the amount of rent scheduled to be paid for schools under conditional and limited transference, and to increase the rent, as fixed in the scheme for schools under absolute trans- ference by about seventy per cent., he can hardly be accused of lack of generosity because he refused to make the grant for maintenance so high that it would put a premium on contracting-out. THE MEETING OF THE CHURCH COUNCIL The extent of the claims for rent and grants put forward by the Church made the hope of agreement appear rather remiee. But the course of the discussion at the Church Council banished hope altogether. It is true that the specific amendments moved by the Bishop of Salisbury were not put to the meeting, and the newspaper reports gave a wrong impression in regard to this. But a majority of the whole Council condemned the Bill outright, though this resolution was not carried, as it failed to command a majority of Bishops. The only alternative, however, which was put forward to a direct repudiation of compro- mise was the Bishop of S ilisbury's motion that the Council could not accept tho Bill unless it received large amendments. Now, the plain man has no difficulty in interpreting such a motion in a stronger sense than that of a mere demand for increased figures in the schedules. But the only amendments suggested were those which the Bishop of Salisbury had indicated on the agenda, though they were not put to the meeting. And some of these amendments struck at the very roots of the agreement which the Government believed that it had arrived at with the Archbishop. The conclusion was irresistible that the Archbishop could not carry his colleagues with him in his efforts for peace. The whole basis of the Bill as an effectual bridge between opposite Iltand- points was swept away. In the meantime the number of Church schools is dwindling week by week. Mr. Balfour has succeeded in tempting Churchmen to lose a precious opportunity by snatching at a shadow. THE DOIIINANT ISSUE. While the fate of the Education Bill was trembling in the balance, attention was diverted for the moment from the issue which really dominates politics to-day. That issue is, of course, the power so ruthlessly exercised by the House of Lords to override the decisions and frustrate the labours of the representa- tives chosen by the public. The House of Lords has destroyed, since the present Government came into office, the Education Bill of 1900, the Plural Voting Bill, the Scottish Holdings Bill, the Scottish Land Valuation Bill, and the Licensing Bill. The destructive energies of the Lords, remarkable as they have been, are surpassed by the blighting influence produced by the knowledge that it is futile to enter- tain any legislative project without taking iuto con- sideration the attitude of the hereditary chamber To say that such a position is intolerable is merely to admit the inefficiency of the English language for adequate description. Party Government has been reduced to a farce, and every Liberal voter is virtually disfranchised by the unfettered activivity of the Lords veto.</p>
        <p>* * The most effective answer to people who are inclined to belittle the Old Ago Pensions Act is to be found in the large number of applications which are con- stantly being dealt with by the pension authorities. The scheme is an excellent beginning and a boon that is being eagerly sought by our toil-worn veterans. At Warrington no fewer than -111 claims have been considered up to date, and the pensions allowed total quite i• 100 per week.lt will be a cause of satisfaction and, indeed, a relief to many that the Local Govern- ment Board has upheld the Warrington Committee's view that Co-operative Society dividend is not to be regarded as income for the purposes of the Act. Had the decision been to the contrary it would have borne harshly upon deserving old people and we are glad that the sensible and humane course which commended itself to the Warrington Committee has found Govern- ment sanction.</p>
        <p>* * The "Manchester Guardian " gives the following item of of recent news from Nanaimo, Vancouver Island, as recorded by the local paper : " Last night Mr. S. Day, a veteran fisherman of tido city, caught a monstrous codfish just outside the harbour, and brought the fish to the city, where it was placed on exhibition. The cod was of extraordinary size. A dissection was ordered, and one of the most remarbable things ever heard of in piscatorial life was brought to life. Inside the big fellow was another codfish some twenty inches in length ; inside this cod was a rock-cod eighteen inches in length, and inside this was another ten inches in length. To make the story complete, inside the innermost cod was the herring which Mr. Day was using as bait. The facts are exactly as stated. The big fish was dissected behind the Hotel Vendome, and the examination was witnessed by a score of citizens." The herring is the only part of this story that any- one needs to believe ; the rest is all cod.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Young People's Society. At a meeting of this society on Monday evening Mr. G. A. Dunlop gave a paper on "Literature and Life," the subject being treated in a very comprehensive and instructive manner. Mr. Dunlop gave some valuable , hints as to what to read and the method of reading.</p>
        <p>For Chi!dram's ttakingC ,a;h at nieht, Wood's Great Peppermint Cars. Is. 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
