      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>NEW REMEDY FOR SEA-SICKNESS</head>
        <p>A NEW REMEDY FOR SEA-SICKNESS</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH BETTING RAID. -- Chemist's Manager Fined.</p>
        <p>A favourable report of four voyagers from Dublin to Holyhead seems to point to the discovery at long and last of an effectual cure and preventive forces-sickness. A short time ago Mr. T. B. Mothersill, a Canadian, I A sequel to the betting raid by the Cheshire County announced that he had discovered the prepar- Police at Stockton Heath on November 13, at the ation for which all those who travel by sea l premises of 66, London-road Stockton Heath, was heard have been searching ever since the first savage at the Daresbary Petty Sessions, on Tuesday, when launched his coracle and was surprised at the discomfort James Vaughan Moore, formerly manager to Mr. Harold which resulted. Three tests wete selected to which to Smith, chemist. 66, Old London-road, was summoned subject the new remedy,and they were admittedly about ' for using his employer's premises for the purpose of the most severe to which a cure for sea-sickness' could have been put. Two ladies and two City betting on ten different dates in October and November. Mr. Giles, of Chester, prosecuted on behalf of the business men, all of whom were in the habit of travel- , police; and Mr. E. A. White defended. ling frequently, and all of whom were very "bad The case for the prosecution woe to the effect that sailors," were selected for the experiment. They were from October 31st to November 13th the premises were carefully watched on the three journeys, the first of under observation by the police. Police-constable which was from Dover to Calais and back, the second Newbrook being disguised Ag a groom. On the latter from Newhaven to Dieppe and back, and the third from d Holyhead to Dublin and back. The last journey in partial- ate the premises were raided by the police, including, lar is a severe test even for the best sailors. Reports to Superintendent Ennion. They there fonrei the defendant i hand state that on all six voyages the cure had proved n company with another man named Endersby and a b named Bridgewater. A search of the persons in a complete success. Mr. Mothersill bad already tested boy the shop revealed certain evidences of betting. In his remedy on the Atlantic with excellent results. Colonel Labelle, who brought over the Canadian answer to a number of qnestions, the boy admitted rifle team to Bisley, treated himself and his men, that he had taken, on the instruction of the defendant, and he declared that they never travelled so ' number of betting slips to Warrington ; and on comfortable before. The America Olympic team also November 12th, an officer, disguised as a groom, went used it, and signed a testimonial to its eflicency. The into the shop, making a purchase and at the same time remedy is in the form of two powders, which are, time handing the defendant a etting slip, on which administered in gelatine capsules. Mr. Mothersil was written the name of a horse, " Vamoose, is. win ; declares that that the contain no cocaine, morphine any back is. rite." This was the only direct bet the opium, or chloral, and have no unpleasant after effects. officer made with the defendant. i Tie has submitted them for analysis to Sir Charles In conclusion, Mr. Giles said it had been arranged Cameron, of Dublin. president of the Society of Public with the defendant's solicitor that if the cases were proved only one penalty should be imposed, and the Analysts, and to a firm of London analysts, and they have failed to discover any traces of alkaloids derived costs paid in respect to the other nine charges. Evidence in sunport of the charges was given by from opium or other alkaloids. They declare that the remedy is entirely of Vegetable origin. Snperintendent Ennion. Sergeant Barlow, and Police Constable Newbrook, Superintendent Ennion stating that in addition to the betting slips found were a number of football connons. Mr. White for the defence said he could not snegest for one moment that Moore had not been mixed up with the betting,but he submitted there was insufficient evidence to prove that he was guilty of the offence with which he was charged. According to the section under which the prosecution was taken, defendant must as a principal, have made the bets, and they had no evidence of that. There was a section expressly to deal with the receiving of money and betting slips, but they were not summoned under that. They had no evidence to show that defendant, as n principal, was making bets and, therefore, using the premises as a betting house ; and he submitted that the Bench were not instified in convicting. Defendant bad now entirely discontinued to have any connection with betting, and he assured him (Mr. White) that he would keep himself free from it in the future. He had already suffered a great amount of punishment. He had lost his situation, he had been arrested and remanded, and had had this banning over him for weeks, and had been put to con- considerable expense. He was at present out of a situation, and had a wife and a mother dependent upon him. If the Bench felt bound to inflict a penalty he asked them to take those things into Recount. The Chairman (Mr. J. C. Parr) said they were quite satisfied that the cases had been proved, and imposed a fine of ClO, including costs. Alfred Lawrence Bridgewater,James Ernest Enderslev and Charles Holmes were charged with using cabyard for the purpose of betting. Bridepa-ater was hmind over. Endersley was fined 40e. and costs, and Holmes 20s. and costs. Mr. J. Loneland, solicitor, said Mr. Harold Smith wished him to state that he entirely dissociated himself from all the proceeding. Moore had been in his employ for over four years. and he was for three or four years in tbe employ of Mr. Smith's predecessor who gave him an excellent character. Mr. Smith had no idea until after tie rail of what had been going en or he would have stopped it at once. Ho repudiated any respon- sibility whatever.</p>
