      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>CHRTSTMS AND NEW YEAR'S HOLIDAY ExcunsioNs.</head>
        <p>CHRTSTMS AND NEW YEAR'S HOLIDAY ExcunsioNs.</p>
        <p>With the near approach of Christmas the nsnal enquiries will be made as to what excursions have been arranged by the various railway companies.. The London and North-Western Railway, as is their custom, ere always to be found to the fore, and a very large , list of cheap trips have been fixed from their Warring- pen (Bank Quay) station. On Christmas and New Year's Eve Bookings will he elven to any parts of Scotland by the West Mast Royal Mail route, for four, six and 18 days. BY the trip on New Year's Eve one I day bookines will be given to Carlisle, Edinboro' and Glasgow. Excursions to London for various periods will be run on Christmas Eve, and Thnrsday afternoon. December 31st. To South Wales, and the West of lEngland, the best and direct route is by the London and North-Western Railway; cheap tickets will be issued for various periods on December 24th. Cheep tickets for three or five days will be issued also on December 24th, to Newcastle, Durham, Sunderland, and other stations on the N.H. line, On December 26th and 31st cheap tickets will Fe issned for various periods to Wolverhampton and Birmingham. Special facilities are also given for those wishing to visit Ireland. Other trips to Manchester, Liverpool, ev,c., are also announced. Full particulars of these are shown on handbills which I may be obtained at the station.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. Wycliffe Young People's Society. At a meeting of this society on Monday evening Mr. G. A. Dunlop gave a paper on "Literature and Life," the subject being treated in a very comprehensive and instructive manner. Mr. Dunlop gave some valuable hints as to what to read and the method of reading. Mr. Harry Serrey. The St. Helens Town Hall was on Monday night crowded when the members of the St. Helens Prize Choir gave a farewell concert to Mr. Harry Berrey, who has been their conductor for the past fourteen years. Mr. Berrey founded the Prize Choir, and quickly drew together a most enthusiastic band of singers, who have won important prizes at all the Lancashire competitions. He is now removing to Warrington. During the past fourteen years many capital vocalists have been discovered by Mr. Berrey, and last night about 150 members rendered a most entertaining and delightful programme. Mr. and Mrs. Berrey were most enthuaiastically received by the crowded audience, and their removal from the town is most sincerely regretted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>FrrocirSu. ALBIONS V. LATCHFORD NITIONA L. —This match was played at Latchford on Saturday. Froghall opened with a fine performance of passing by Marsh, Kennyaston and Harding, which resulted in Kennyaston placing the leather between the posts. Soon after this Kennyaston agsin beat the Latchford custodian. The visitors again scored through Harding, who took up Marsh's forward pass,outwitted Peacock,ran coolly in and heat the National custodian with a fine cross shot. The Nationalists responded at once with a spirited attack, but the strong defence of Dakirrand Worthington could not be passed, and Worthington with a vigorous kick sent the ball to Kennyaston, who forced another goal. Soon after Nfarsh scored from a forward pass from Fiearnan. A fine dribble by Pugh and then a smart pass from Hare resulted in Harding placing the ball into the net. Half-time : Froghall, six goals ; National, nil. Tn the second half Kennyaston added two more goals to his credit while Harding scored one by taking a cross kick from Goodwin. Ten minutes off time Peacock taking a forward pass from Davies shot, but Horrabin cleared, the ball rebounding from Davies to Bradbury who shot a yard from the goal, and the ball, skimming the cross bar, drooped between the posts. Final : Froghall 9 goals, Latchford 1 goal. FROGH ILT. ALMONS V. ALL SkINTS.—(At Bowsey). Team: H. S. Horrahin ; J. Deakin and T. Worthington; H. Beaman, A. Cotterill and W. Hare ; .T. Marsh, 11. Kennyaston, H. Harding, H. Goodwin and IT. Pugh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>IN PAIN AFTER EVERY MEAL.</head>
        <p>IN PAIN AFTER EVERY MEAL.</p>
        <p>A Lay's 6 Years' Terrible Indigestion. -- Cured by Chas. FORBES Bile Beans.</p>
        <p>Miss Ada Mellins, a young governess living at Elmhurst, Chart Lane, Reigate, says :—" For about six years' I was a martyr to indigestion. After meals I had sharp pains at the pit of my stomach, and every- thing I ate repeated for some time afterwards. The slightest amount of food caused my face to flush and get very heated. Even a dr;nk of milk gave me terrible pains in the chest and side, and the agony was increased until it became so fearful I was afraid to eat.</p>
        <p>" Many tunes I was awakened in the night by pains in the back and a stifling, choking feeling at the chest. I tried many doctors, but their remedies did me no good whatever. I gradually became thin, weak and lowly depressed. A few days after trying Chas. Forde's Bile Beans I felt refreshed, and I thoroughly enjoyed some food without any rain following. I persevered with Chas. Forde's and p'cked up strength rapidly. I was able to sleep and woke up refreshed. Meal-times are now a pleasure instead of a torture, as the indigestion pain has never returned. lam now unite cured, thanks entirely to Chas. Forde's Bile Beans."</p>
        <p>Miss Melling was cured by the genuine "Chas. Forde's " Bile Beans. Beware of worthless " cheap " substitutes. The ORNCINE are sold only in sealed wooden boxes at Is. I id. and !s. Od., and says 'CHARLES FORDE'S " on the label,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Discussion On Christmas Beer.</p>
        <p>BIG BUILDING SCHEME IN PROSPECT.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, Mr. G. Mather presiding. There were also present : Mrs. Simpson, Miss Broadbent, Rev. M. Ryan, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, W. Gill, \V. C. Brooks, W. Furness. E. L. Bent, R. Lee, W. Houghton, W. Flanagan, W. Stanier, R. Forshaw, J. Backhouse, A. Brooks, R. Bolton, M. W. Seton, J. Evans A. Bottomley (clerk), It. W. Peacock (assistant clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of the Cottage Homes). THE PRIVATE FUND. The minutes of the last meeting having been read, Mr. BENT said he would like to know by whose authority the private fund account was published in the Press. The CHAIRMAN: You had better ask me something else. Mr. BENT : I don't think it is exactly right that this account should be published. I don't think there is anything wrong, but the public generally are led to</p>
