.does it cost £10? weight against the purchaser.—A servant girl to the Mr. HOUGHTON : Oh, you are a brewer. (Laughter.) defendant was called and said she put the coin there 1 Mr. W. C. BROOKS remarked that the beer would not on one occasion as the counter was covered with cost that much. boxes, and forgot to take it off again. She never 1 The CHAIRMAN: I see the Chairman of the Com- mittee has power to purchase. I don't know whether put the money in the till, but always gave it to her ,he tastes it or not. (Laughter.) mistress.--The bench ordered defendant to pay the i Mr. FORSHAW : Does it cost 10s. ?</p>
        <p>I The CHAIRMAN: Ob, yes. 1 Mr. FORSHAW : We want some idea of the cost. The CH AIRMAN : The Clerk has not asked for prices yet. If I had wished to ask anybody what the beer is ' likely to cost, I should have asked the member for Burtonwood as knowing more about the price of beer than anyone else. Mr. BACKHOUSE : What is the quantity per inmate? Mr. FORSHAW : Is the statement right that most of the inmates are here through beer ? Mr. HOUGHTON : Has anything gone wrong through beer being supplied at the Christmas dinners during the past two or three years? The CHAIRMAN (to the master) : What has been the conduct of the inmates on Christmas Day since you became master ? The MASTER : Their conduct has been exemplary. The Ctr k mar AN : Do they look happy when they have had their dinner? The MASTER : Personally I skould very much regret to see the Christmas dinner served in this Work- house without a glass of beer being given to the inmates. The CLERK stated that the quantity purchased was 40 dozen one pint bottles and 15 dozen half-pint bottles. Mr. A. BROOKS : I take it, it is not a matter of cost, but of principle. Mr. FORSHANY : It is a matter that anybody raising should be prepared with facts. Miss BROADEENT said she did not think Mrs. Simpson was thinking of the cost, nor did she wish to spoil the pleasure of the inmates on Christmas Day. What she had in mind was that in some cases it was no kindness to give beer as it would probably re-awaken the craving. The voting was as follows :—For the amendment : Mrs. Simpson, Miss Broadbent, Mr. A. Brooks-3. Against : Rev. M. Ryan, Messrs. Backhouse, Bent, Bolton, W. C. Brooks, Flanagan, Forshaw, Furness,Gill, Houghton, Jolley, Seton, Staniar and the Chairman-14. Mr. R. Lee did not vote. The amendment was lost and the beer will therefore be supplied as usual. BIG EXTENSION SCHEME. At the request of the House Committee Mr. Seger Owen, architect, appeared before the Board to explain the scheme for the erection of a new infirm block which had been under the consideration of that committee. The CHAIRMAN said that at the present time the institution was overcrowded or quite full. The pres- sure was evidenced by the fact that they had had to buy 52 extra bedsteads. The hospital, the house, and Padgate schools were all full. Two or three years ago they got Mr. 0 yen to prepare plans of a new infirm block, and they were passed by the Local Government Board and estimates were obtained. At the lest moment, however, the Board in their wisdom thought they could put off the scheme for some time and so it had continued up to the present. At the meeting of the committee the point was raised as to whether the result of the Royal Commission on the Poor LAW now sitting would make any difference to their accommoda- tion. Another suggestion WAS as to whether it would be better to build a lunatic block and thus relieve the pressure on the hospital, part of which was at present occupied by female lunatics. The male lunatic wards were in a dilapidated condition. As the scheme for a new infirm block had been submitted before and the consent of the Local Government Board obtained, the Committee thought it would be the best to bring before the Board. Mr. JOLLEY seconded the missing of the minutes. The CLERic stated that the Board would not bind themselves to the erection of the buildings that morn- ing. At the meeting on January sth the following resolutions would be considered :—That the Guardians hereby consent : (1) To the erection at the Workhouse of the Union of buildings for the accommodation of 1 the infirm in accordance with plans to which the Local eliovernment Board have affixed their seal, subject nevertheless to such modifications thereof as that Board may hereafter direct. (2) To the provision of furniture for the said buildings. (3) To the expenditure for the above purposes of a sum not exceeding £5,430. (3) To the borrowing of the said sum of £8,430 or such part thereof as may be necessary for the purposes aforesaid. Mr. SEG AR OWEN then explained the plans in detail and said the cost would be as follows :—Cost of structure, £7,050 ; boundary walls and fencing, £lOO ; digging carting and levelling, £125; furnishing generally, £550; architects' charges, £454 l3s. (of which £lO9 2s. ad. has already been paid) ; cost of raising loan, £5O ; contingencies, £lOO ; total £8,129 13s. Mr. Owen stated that this figure included the cost of heating and lighting the building. Mr. Jor.f..Eir warmly protested against a remark by Mr. Bolton that the scheme would probably cost twice as much as estimated. It was utterly baseless and untrue. Mr. BENT said he was satisfied the amount required was not extravagant, considering that it included beating, lighting and furnishing. He moved that the Clerk be authorised to proceed with the necessary preliminaries, and inform the Local Government Board that the Guardians were again considering the matter. Mr. IlottairroN seconded and the resolution was carried. The minutes of the committee were approved. SALARY ADVANCES. At a meeting of the Finance Committee it was recom- mended that the salaries at the under-mentioned officers be increased as follows :—Mr. John Jones, from £29 Bs. to £34 Bs. per annum ; Mr. Fred Yates from £29 Bs. to £34 Bs. : Miss Emily Bennett from £l9 3s. to £2O ; Miss Alice Bennett from £2O to £2l ; Miss Sarah A. Warburton from £2O to £2l. It was recommended that increases of the salaries of the undermentioned officers</p>
        <p>costs, 3s. 6d. League of Pity. The annual meeting of the Warrington Branch of the Children's League of Pity was held at the Winmarleigh Cafe, Sankey-street, on Saturday afternoon. There was a moderate attendance of members. Miss Broadbent presided and gave a hearty welcome to all present. Ira the course of the afternoon, Mrs. Frank Bush gave a lecture on the good work the League was doing and also gave some excellent plans as to the collection of subscriptions. Mrs. Bush advised the members to do all they could to relieve the suffering of the poor children of the country and that could only be done by collecting sums of money in order to provide homes for them. The lecturer gave many instances of the suffering of poor children and also the good work the N.S.P.C.C. is doing for country. She stated that their society in Warrington bad collected £lO 3s. as compared with £9 last year. The dog collector" Peter Pan " had collected £1 4s. The members afterwards partook of an excellent tea, provided by the Winmarleigh Cafe, Co.iLtd., and a pleasant afternoon was spent. LINSEED COMPOUND 'for Coughs and Col ds. Of proven efficacy. Of Chemists only. 91(1.,15.1id.,22.9d.-Advt Milton Tercentenary. A temporary exhibition of early editions of John Milton's works is at present on view at the Warrington Museum, in commemoration of the tercentenary of the birth of the great pet. A few notes may add to their interest. (1) Poems of Mr. John Milton both English and Litia, 1615, 12mo. This is one of the rarest of Milton's works. The second edition (2) was published in 1073. (3) Joannis Miltuni Angli pro Populo Anglicano Defensio secunda, 1054, 12mo. This Second Defence of the people of England was one of a series of controversial pamphlets. The First Defence is represented (4) by the edition of 1692. (5) Accedence Commenc't Grammar . . . J.M., 1669, ISmo, is a little introduction to Latin, and is a quaint illustration of what was cousirlered a helpful primer in the 17th ceniury. (0) The Ilistory of Britain, that part especially now called England, 1670, sm. 4to. A second edition (7) appeared in 12mo. seven year later. (8) Paradise Lost, a poem in twelve books. The second edition, 1761, 12°. The first edition, of which there were many differing " issues" was in ten books only. The third edition (9) appeared in 1078, 12mo. In addition to these examples of Milton's works the case contains a few others connected. Toland's Life of Milton, 1699, was one of the earliest memoirs of the poet. A. facsimile of the family register in Milton's Bible gives a good specimen of his handwriting. Mr. Fitchett Marsh, of Warrington, having come into possession of some manuscrints relating to Milton, was led to investiga- tion which resulted in the publication of several papers, which are shown. These inclade papers connected with the affairs of Milton and his family (Chetham Society, 1851) ; Notice of the Inventory of the effects of Mrs. Mi'ton, widow of the Poet (Historic Society, 1855) ; and On the Engraved Portraits of Milton Historic Society, 1860). Table, Floor, and Hanging LA.alrs. Latest designs now showing. See windows.—J. S. GARLAND, 51- 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Oddfelloviship. There was a large representative attendance from the various Oddfellows lodges in the Warrington district on Saturday evening to mitness the initiation of Councillor W. B. Roberts as an honorary member tof the " Travellers' Rest" Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity). The pro- ceedings took place at the Mulbery Tree Hotel, Stockton Heath. Bros. Sir Gilbert Glreenall, Bart„ and R. Pierpoint wrote regretting their inability to be present. The chair was occupied by Provincial Grand Master Lot Brooks, who was supported by various officers The initiation was impressively performed by P.P.G.U. Massey, after which the health of the 1:41w honorary member was proposed in felicitous terms by P.P.G.M. T. P. Bradshaw. If Bro. Councillor Roberts would find time occasion- ally to visit that lodge he would see that much good was beinr2 done and that Oddlellowslaip had a tendency to make better husbands, fathers, and better members of society. They all hoped to see him in the near future occupying the proud position of chief magistrate of Warrington. (llear,hear,and applause.) The toast having been cordially drunk, Bro Councillor Roberts br'efly responded. Songs were well rendered by Bros. Alban Boyle, Lot Brooks, J. MacLacialan, Fred Warburton and T. Reddish. A recitation by Bro. J. Massey caused roars of laughter, and Bro. T. Reddish with his song, " Finnigan's wakes," scored great success. A vote of thanks was accorded the Deputy Grand Master (Bro. G. F. Freeman) for making the arrangements of the evening, and to the artistes and pianist for their services.—Bros. Freeman and Fred. Webster replied.</p>
        <p>.1</p>
        <p>be not granted :—Mrs. Eliza Jones, Mrs. Louisa Yates, I and Mr. Oswald H. Moss. The minutes were approved. UNEMPLOYMENT. The Tottenham Urban District Council wrote asking the Board to endorse a resolution urging that the unemployed problem could only be satisfactorily dealt with on a national basis and calling upon the Govern- ment to take any necessary steps to deal with the problem in a national and systematic manner by them- selves undertaking works of national necessity, such as afforestation reclamation of fore-shores and waste land, and the institution of farm colonies &amp;c. Mr. BENT : I think we might let it lie on the table. I move that. Mr. JoLLEv seconded. Mr. FoRSHAW : I am sick of fore-shores Stc. (Laughter ) The proposition was agreed to. CHRISTMAS RELIEF' The CLERK intimated that the Sheffield and Doncaster Unions allowed extra out-relief during Christmas week and they wanted permission to pay the larger amount , to people chargeable to the Warrington Union who were living in those places. The CHAIRMAN : This Board has never done any- thing of the sort. The CLERIC : Unless you allow it to your own poor it would be giving an advantage to those persons chargeable to you and residing in other Unions. The BOARD agreed with this view and declined to accede to the request. MEDICAL RELIEF AND OLD-AGE PENSIONS. IMPORTANT POINT. Mr. JAMES EVANS said that in discussing applica- tions at the meeting of the Warrington Pensions Com- mittee the previous day,a case ca 410 up in which a man was admitted from the Warrington Infirmary to the Union Hospital. The question naturally arose as to whether the man became chargeable to the funds of the Union. The CLERIC: Yes. Mr. EVANS said he did not think persons should be sent from the Infirmary to the Union Hospital and because of that fact lose their pension aud in all probability become a permanent charge to the funds of the Union. The CLERK : I don't think the man will lose his pension. They have provided in the Old Age Pensions Act for medical relief. Mr. EVANS : That is quite true but it becomes com- plicated because the technical point largely depends upon what doctor ,ordered the person to receive such medical assistance. We ought to know definitely upon what lines a person is removed from the Infirmary to this hospital and by which medical officer's authority. If for the relief of the Infirmary a man is ordered to the Workhouse ITospital and is thus to be debarred from his pension, it becomes a serious matter and we ought to say something about it. It may affect a large number of persons. The CHAIRMAN : It has been held by the revenue authorities that anybody coming into a Poor Law Infirmary receives relief in the shape of food, and that is quite different to having relief outside. Mr. EVANS : The revenue officers are taking up that position and there is nothing for it but an appeal to the Local Government Board for a decision. My point is that this Board should exactly understand on what lines persons are transferred from any institution that might jeopardise the receipt of a pension. Mr. FORSHAW : It is a question for the Old Age Pensions Committee. So far as we are concerned it is more or less academic. The CLERK : It is entirely a question for the Old Age Pensions Committee. Mr. A. BROOKS : Does not the fault lie with the Infirmary Committee and not with us? If a man is sent here destitute we are obliged to take him in, either in one department or another. If any member of the Board is interested in this, could he not approach the Infirmary Committee and point out to them the dis- abilities which a man sent in under certain circumstances will incur, and ask them to be as lenient as possible ? Mr. EVANS : I think that will be done. Mr. BROOKS ! Ido not see that it is a matter for us , as Guardians, because whoever sends a destitute person , here, he must be taken in. Mr. JOLLEY thought the word "misfortune " would , be more appropriate than " fault " in this matter. They , had no reason, without evidence before them, to suppose , that anybody had done anything wrong. It must be I borne in mind that the Warrington Infirmary was not , provided for the purpose of relieving the Union , Hospital of its responsibilities. If there was any fault it was in the state of the law and not in the action of • any individual. • The matter then dropped. THE LATE SIR J. T. 'HEBERT. Mr. HOUGHTON said he would like to see in the new , Infirmary some fitting memorial to the late Sir J. T. , llibbert, who did them the honour of opening the • building. They had no means of doing it but by private subscription and he hoped the members would think the matter over.</p>
