      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>GREAT FEMALE REMEDY.</head>
        <p>A GREAT FEMALE REMEDY.</p>
        <p>ASHTON-UNDER-LYNE LADY'S STRONG RECOMMENDATION.</p>
        <p>Throughout the many stages of woman's life from peevish girlhood, through womanhood, motherhood, to the declining years of old age, there is no better, milder, or more effective</p>
        <p>medical companion to women-folk than Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills. They promote healthy and regular visitations, heal inflamma- tions and ulceration, and cure all womanly weaknesses, cleansing and restoring the Bowels, Liver, and Kidneys to perfect working order. A few doses occasionally, when well, will assure a pure and healthy condition. ' I am pleased to inform you," writes Mrs. M. Holland, of 13, Dale Street, Ashton-under-Lyne, " that I have used Dr. Morse's jndian Root Pills and they have proved most satisfactory, and I feel that they are doing me a great amount of good. I shall continue to take them, feeling sure that they will eventually cure me. I shall always recommend Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills. As a searching, cleansing remedy they are the very best medicine that I have ever taken, and I shall always recommend them as a splendid Family Remedy." Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills get at the cause by cleansing the stomach, purifying the blood, and aiding the- digestion and assimilation of food, and are a positive and permanent cure for Indigestion' Biliousness, Constipation, Impure Blood, and Female Ailments. Sold by Chemists and Stores, price 1/11, per bottle, or The W. H. Comstock Co., Ltd., 21, Farringdon Avenue, London, E.C.</p>
        <p>Mir I was very pleased to receive a letter front the Head Mistress of a large school in- forming me that the scholars had a parcel of clothing ready for my poor little children. Don't you think this an excellent ideal The children,had been making these clothes dur- ing the last few weeks, and now, as Christ- mas is approaching, the parcel is despatched to help me in my work. I must thank most heartily the kindness of the lady in taking such an interest in the matter. 4',</p>
        <p>I had a very pretty card from one of our members, James Lister. It is hand painted, and reflects great credit on the painter. Well, children, I hope you are getting on well with the Competitions. My letter is short this week, as the printers are wanting the copy.</p>
        <p>With love, UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>Welcome to Mary Holt. I LIKE CRICKET BEST, AND WHY.</p>
        <p>I like cricket best because it is a very healthy exercise. It is played in summer be- cause winter is too cold. Sometimes we have to stand out in the field a long time before the ball reaches us, so that it wouldn't be wise to play at all in winter. There is not so much running about in cricket as football. Football is too exciting, and always makes you hot and breathless, which sometimes brings on illness. I think cricket is the best of summer sports: it is such a cool and good exercise for the muscles. WILFRED PRIEST.</p>
        <p>-4--</p>
        <p>CRICKET AND FOOTBALL-WHICH I PREFER, AND WHY.</p>
        <p>Football is one of the most exciting and amusing outdoor games in winter. Cricket is a game which is generally played in summer. It takes more money to provide things to play at a game of cricket than football, as only a ball and goalpcists are needed to play at foot- ball, whereas wickets, bails, bats, balls, knee- pads, and gloves for the hands are needed to play at cricket, and this does not keep you so warm as playing at football. I think I like football the better of these two games, as there is more 'running and jumping about in it than cricket.. The game' of cricket is more dangerous than that of football, as the ball with which you play at cricket is much harder than that with which you play at foot- ball. If a cricket ball is sent very swiftly, and it hits a person, it hurts him very much, and sometimes renders him unconscious. When a football is lost, it caà easily be found, as it is about six times larger than a cricket ball, and it can easily be seen. When you lose a cricket ball, it is hard to find, as it is so small in size. The game of football generally com- mences at the end of September or the early part of October. GERALD APDLEBY (9 years).</p>
        <p>.'ERENCE !WEN A RICD! CHILD'S CHRISTMAS AND A POOR CHILD'S CHRISTMAS.</p>
        <p>All children look out for the coming of Christmas. The poor child's Christmas is sometimes no 'different than at any other time, especially if its father and mother are drunkards. They will stop out all night on Christmas Eve, and leave the child to mind the house. They think nOthing about the child's pleasure; they spend all their money, and the child can cry as much as it likes for what they care. It has to be content with nothing. The rich child's Christmas is a very happy one. It is put to ked soon on Christmas Eve. Father and mother tell it Father Christmas is coming. When it wakens in the morning' it finds its stocking full of toys, and its heart leaps with joy. It is, perhaps, the only child they have, and it is the darling of the home. It eats as much plum pudding as it wants, and spends a jolly evening at home with as little friends. We all ought to be thankful we have good fathers and mothers. Wishing you and ' all your nephews and nieces a happy Christmas, ERtiEST DONEY.</p>
        <p>-4.---</p>
        <p>A BROTHER'S LOVE.</p>
        <p>'Tis Christmas Eve, and, oh! how cold the night is; it makes one feel how much more comfortable it is to stay indoors, and sit in front of the fire, with a story-book on my knee. But, no, I must go out to-night to buy a present for my little brother, so that I may give him a Christmas gift. Putting on my overcoat and muffler, I make my way out into the night, being well protected from the cold. Most of the shop windows are decked out in Christmas splendour, and I am filled with ad- miration as I see the fine things therein dis- played. I have a great difficulty in choosing a present most suitable for my brother, and whilst meditating in front of a Large shop my attention is drawn to a little ragged urchin (who has a few unsold newspapers under his arm), busy counting his coppers, and I heard him mutter to himself: "I won'er how muclt that horse is." I looked at the object his gaze was fixed upon in the shop window, and I saw a wooden horse, which would cost more than the price he had muttered. After stand- ing there awhile, he mustered up his cour- age, and slowly walked into the shop, and he asked the gentleman behind the counter the price of the horse in the window, and, being told it was a shilling, he looked very sad in- deed. With tears in his eyes, he told the shop- man, that he had only got sixpence, and he sadly turned away from the counter. Now the shopkeeper was known to be a thoroughly Christian gentleman, and, seeing the boy's de- jected look, he thought of his own boys and girls at home, of all the good things they would find in the morning. " Here, lad, take the horse. No, never mind the money—and a happy Christmas to thee." The boy's pleasure knew no bounds, so, 'with grateful thanks, he took the horse under his arm and ran out of the shop and into the street. But in his joy he had failed to notice a motor-car coming swiftly down the street, and before I could warn him he was knocked down by the motor. I went along with him to the infirmary, and the doetor found that he was badly hurt, and he would have to stay there for a few weeks. The poor boy's tears fell fast when he 'heard the doctor say this, so I asked him if I could do anything for him, and he thanked me, and said, ain't got no father nor mother—only a little brother, who is poorly in bed. We lives wiv an ole woman, an' I've promised my little brother to tell Santa Claus to bring him a horse, and now I canna take it. Will yer please take it, and put it on his bed when ee's asleep?" I gave him my word that I would do as he wished. He gave me the address, and I made my way out into the snow, which had begun to fall rather thickly. The hour was rather late, and, with difficulty. I at last found the house, in a slum quarter of the town. I knocked at the door, but, re- ceiving no answer, I lifted the latch, ail walked in, when suddenly I heard a child's voice repeating the Lord's Prayer. He went through the whole of it, and then finished up with these words: "Dear Jesus, don't let Santa Claus forget my horse." I waited a little longer, until he was asleep, then, placing the horse upon his bed, I left that house with a very sad heart, but fully determined that those two brothers should be better cared for this Christmas than they had ever been before.</p>
        <p>WILLLOI R. FARRAR.</p>
