      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <p>-I</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—Football or Cricket, which I like best. In answering the above question I have no hesitation whatever in saying foot- ball. Played when it is coldest, only can we keep warm by exerting ourselves and keeping on the move. What a sense of exhilaration comes on us as, with ball at toe, and a clear run ahead we rush down the field I How glorious it is to score' a goal, a gloriousness for the moment equalled by nothing else I can conceive of, certainly not by a catch at cricket, however good it may be, or even a drive to boundary. In arguing for cricket what can be brought forward as outweighing the excitement with which is awaited the result of a corner being talcen, a penalty kick, or the end of a brilliant run down the field? Yes, beside these things cricket falls flat, to my mind seems " tame " and slow. Again, for a minute, let us consider the moral benefits we derive from playing football. Selfishness is a thing unknown on the football field. With the ball at one's feet what a mighty temptation it is to run down the field alone, risking the loss of the ball for one's own self-gratification in scoring a goal/ To act thus may be our inclination the first few times we play, but getting better acquainted with the game we are taught that would we be successful in the game we must play the "passing game," and not work on our own. Thus being taught to practice unselfishness on the playing fields, surely we shall carry the effects of it into our after daily life, and be less/selfish than before. Perseverance we also learn on the football field. Our first attack failing; more firmly does it become rooted in our minds to make a more corn- bined and strenuous effort, and this failing I yet another, having the while ft* our motto:— I "If at first you don't succeed, _.</p>
        <p>Try try, try again." . . .</p>
        <p>I think I have fairly outlined the chief reasons why I like football better than cricket, yet by no means in detail, for far more space than a letter allows,.-would it require to do justice .to the subject.'</p>
        <p>HUBEET H. HAYES.</p>
        <p>Physician (to Government clerk): "Well, what do you complain of I" " Sleeplessness, doctor." "At what time do you-go to bed?" "Oh I I don't mean at night, but during office hours."</p>
        <p>Dobbs: "Maria, that beast of a dog of yours must go. She has just kitten a piece out of the calf of my leg."</p>
        <p>Maria: "oh, this is too terrible." Dobbs: "it is a comfort to have some sympathy for once." Maria: "I was not thinking of you at all; but the veterinary surgeon yesterday ordered poor Florrie to be restricted to a milk diet."</p>
