      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>POSTAL AND SAVINUS BANK REGULATIONS</head>
        <p>POSTAL AND SAVINUS BANK REGULATIONS</p>
        <p>For further particulars see "Post Office Guide," at any Post (Mice. POSTAGE STAMPS. Books containing 12 penny and 23 halfpenny stamps are now on sale at each post office. Price 2s.</p>
        <p>INLAND CORRESPONDENCE. _</p>
        <p>The term "Inland Correspondence" means cor- respondence posted within the United Kingdom, including the Isle of Man and the Channel Islands. "Inland Correspondence" is divided into the following classes : (a) Letters (d) Newspapers (h) Postcards (e) Parcels (c) Halfpenny packets Not only letters, but any document,or article (with a few trifling exceptions) which can be trans- mitted by post at all, may be sent by letter post, the only limitation being that of size.</p>
        <p>LETTEEL POST. The prepaid rate of postage is as follows : Not exceeding 4 ozs. in weight ... Id. For every additionai 2 0z5....</p>
        <p>A letter posted unpaid is chargeable on delivery with double postage; a letter insufficiently paid, with double the deficiency. No letter may exceed two feet in length, one foot in width, or one foot in depth. POST-CARDS.</p>
        <p>The prepaid rate of postage on every post-card is one half-penny, and on every reply post-card one penny. A post-card posted unpaid ie chargeable oa delivery with one penny. Cards used as post-cards must be made of ordinary card-board, not thicker than the material used for the thickest official post-card. They may not exceed 51 inches in length by 31 inches in width, or be less than 31 inches in length by 21 inches in width. A gummed label (not to exceed 2 inches long and 3-inch wide) bearing the address may now be affixed to the address side of card. If any of the rules relating to a post-card be infringed, the card will be treated as a letter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>iii"I.EPENNY PACKET POST.</head>
        <p>iii"I.EPENNY PACKET POST.</p>
        <p>The Halfpenny Packet Post can be used only for packets not exceeding 20z. in weight. Printed matter, Sm., not in the nature of a letter, may be transmitted by this method. NEWSPAPER POST.</p>
        <p>The prepaid rate of PO-a-titie --on--; -registered news- paper, including any supplement thereto, whether posted singly or with others is id. Newspapers not registered at the General Post Office are treated in the post like any other printed matter. PARCEL POST. The rates of postage on parcels areas follows: For a parcel not exceeding 1 lb. 0 3 yy exceeding 1 lb. 2 lb. 0 4 2 lb pi • 3 113. 0 5 • ff 3 lb. 1, 5 lb. 0 6 If 110 5 lb. 7 lb. 0 7 )9 yy 7 lb. 8 lb. 0 8 „ 8 lb. 9 lb. 0 9 „ 9 lb. 10 lb. 010 ~ 10 lb. 11 lb. 011 No parcel may exceed 111 b. in weight. The size allowed for an inland parcel is:— Greatestlength... ... 3 ft. 6 in. Greatest length and girth combined 6 ft. 0 in. For example—A parcel measuring 3ft. Gin. in its longest dimensions as much as 2ft. 6in, round its thickest part ; a short parcel may be thicker ; thus, if it measure no more than 31t. in length, it may measure as much as 3ft. round its thickest part. The postage on a parcel must be prepaid. Mark each parcel Parcel Post." A parcel must not be posted in a letter box, but presented at the counter of a post office, and this sender should see that the weight, size and postage are in order before leaving. PARCELS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL). Parcels are accepted for parcel post to most foreign countries under conditions similar to the inland regulations, except that for customs require- ments the contents must be declared. The customs charges can be prepaid or collected on delivery. The postage varies to different countries and must be prepaid. MONEY ORDERS (INLAND.) The postal authorities cannot issue a single money order for more than £4O, and will only cash them at the money order office at which they are made payable, unless they are crossed, when they can be paid through a bank. No fractional part of a id. can be contnined in a money order. The rates of poundage are :—Not exceeding £l, 2d. ;£3, 3d.; £lO, 4d.; £2O, Gd. ; £3O, Bd. ; £4O, 10d. By paying an additional 2d. and cost of the telegram of advice, a money order can be telegraphed under certain restrictions. MONEY ORDERS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL.) The maximum amount for which a money order may be issued for places abroad varies in each particular place. The poundage rates are :—Not exceeding £l,3d. ; £2, Gd. ; and above that, 3d. for each additional £2 or portion of it. A fee of 6d. in addition to the ordinary poundage, together with cost of telegram of advice, enables money orders to be telegraphed to certain places abroad. POSTAL ORDERS. These are obtainable at any money order office in the United Kingdom, and possess the advantage over money orders that they can be cashed at any money order office. If crossed, they can be paid into a bank. They are issued for fixed amounts, rising by ad., from 6d. to Is. The poundage fees are :—ld. up to 2s. Gd.; Id, nu to 155.; lid. up to 21s. Before parting with a postal order, always fill in the name of the person to whom the amount is to be paid. Each order is numbered, as is also the counterfoil attached, which latter should be retained and name of payee filled in. The Post- master-General will entertain no further claim after an order is once paid. Stamps to the amount of sd. can be affixed to make up odd sums.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>TELEGRAMS (INLAND).</head>
        <p>TELEGRAMS (INLAND).</p>
        <p>The Postmaster-General posssesses,tilmost entirely, the privilege of transmitting,collecting and deliver- ing telegrams in the United Kingdom. The charge for a telegram is a minimum amount of 6d., which covers 12 words, and an additional id. for each extra word. This charge includes delivery within the postal district if sent out for delivery from a head telegraph office, but if from a sub-office then within three miles only, beyond which a charge of 3d. per mile is made from the office door. All charges are, as far as possible, prepared by affixing stamps to telegram form. Address of person receiv- ing a telegram has to be paid for, but sender's name if written on back of form need not be. Five figures count as one word. 'r he cost of a reply may bo prepaid. Telegrams can be repeated on payment of half the original charge, which extra payment is refunded if error is detected. Telegrams may be posted in wall or pillar boxes, or handed to rural postmen, and are wired as soon as received at the nearest telegraph office. Late fees secure attention, in certain cases to telegrams handed in after the usual hours. The cost of registering an abbrevi- ated address, which must consist of two words, is Li is. TELEGRAMS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL) Must ho written on special forms enders accepted in the language of any of the principal European countries. The rates vary, and are too numerous to detail here. POST OFFICE SAVINGS RANK. ' These are established at all money order offices. In ordinary cases the annual deposit must not exceed .E5O, and the total amount £2OO. No person can have more than one account. Amounts placed in the bank on behalf of children under seven years of age cannot be withdrawn until the child reaches that age, and then only under his or her own signa- ture. The smallest amount received at a time is Is., and this may take the form of 12 penny stamps affixed to a special form. Each depositor receives a batik book in which the amount of each deposit should be entered at the time of paying in. If the deposit is £1 or over, a special acknowledgment is sent from the head London office. Interest is allowed at the rate of 2i per cent. Any amount up to and including .£t may be withdrawn without previous notice, on presenting a hank book. The bank book should be sent each year, postage free, on the anniversary of the date when the account was opened, to the Controller of the Savings Bank Department for purposes of checking and adding interest. The withdrawal of money can hoe xpedited by telegraphing, the depositor paying cost of telegrams. P.O. TELEPHONE AND TRUNK LINE CHARGES. The fees for the use of the Trunk lines are as follows:— For 25 miles or under ... If f" lf If ..• (id. If 75 n 9d. „ 100 , ',,,,every additional 40 miles or fraction thereof... 6d. Between the hours of 7 p.m. and 7 a.m., six minutes conversitlon is allowed for the same price as three minutes between 7 a.m. and 7 p.m., and a reduced charge is made for three minute calla.</p>
        <p>MOTOR CAR AND CYCLS REM:MATIONS. Each one must be registered with the Council of. a county or county borough, the fee being .C 1 lured motor car, and ss. for a motor cycle. A register a number is assigned to each, which it is an offence not to keep aflixecl when driving. Each driver nmst possess a license, costing 55., to be renewed every 12 months, end obtainable from the county authcrities, which may 143 endorsed or suspended for reckless driving or certain other offences. _ . .</p>
        <p>The highest speed at which a car may be driven on the public highways is 20 miles an hour, which is reduced to 10 miles under specila local regula- tions. It is an offence under the Nlcter Car Act (1003), to drive a car in such a way on a public, highway as to be dangerous to the public, having regard to ail the circumstances of the case. In the event of an accident to any person- due to be presence of the motor car on the road, the river must supply his name and address, and the twner and number of the car, under heavy penal- ()die The driver must produceh is licence at the sequest of a police constable.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday December 12, 1908.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>For ag'st P. Vir. L. D. G. G. P. Earlestown St. John's 10 8 2 0 41 9 16 Lymm Rylands Bartonwood</p>
        <p>W'gton Albions Res. 4 3 1 0 13 8 6 Latchford Whitecross Orford Halton</p>
        <p>--- Burtonwood Wesleyan 7 1 6 0 9 16 2 SECOND DIVISION. E'town St. John's Res. 7 7 0 0 23 1 14 Burtonwood Reserve 6 5 1 0 17 6 10 Newton Amateurs 6 4 2 0 12 7 8 Whitecross Reserve Thelwall United *l4lands Reserve 9 3 5 1 16 17 5 Walker's Lymm Reserve Frodsham Brotherhood 7 0 5 2 5 20 2 *Two points deducted for playing an ineligible man. Halton Reserve withdrawn. Record expunged. Matches for December 12th.</p>
        <p>2nd Division Rylands Reserve v. Newton Amateurs. WARRINGTON " OBSERVER " CUP—SECOND ROUND.</p>
        <p>Castner Kellner v. Halton Villa. Latchford v. Barnton Villa. Lymm v. Burtonwood Wesley. Sutton Commercial v. Whitecross. Warrington Albions v. Halton. Earlestown St. John's v. Clock race. Walker's v. Marston United. Burtonwood V. Rylands. Results for December sth. Ist Division :—Orford 3, Whitecross 2. 2nd Divisicn :—Frodshatn Brotherhood 1, Thelwall United 1, Ryli.nds Reserve 4, Walkers 2.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL SHIELD. THIRD ROUND.</p>
        <p>_ WARRINOTON ALI3ION V. SKELMERSDALE UNITED.— Great interest was taken in this match which was played on the ground of the Albion on Saturday. United started and were quickly defending. Pendlebury raced away and from a grand centre Ball gave Warrington the lead. The Albion continued to hold the upper hand,but Skelmersdale attacked and Wyatt cleared. A penalty to the United and the teams were equal. The Albions were giving the United defence a warm time and forced a corner, which was cleared. A corner to United made no alteration to the score, but Skeltnersdale took the lead later, but the Albion persevered and Hunter put on the equalizing point before ends were changed. The second half was brilliantly contested,the Warringtonians showing good form throughout and Dutton placing a goal, the locals won a well deserved victory by three goals to two. _</p>
        <p>I</p>
