      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>LANCASHIRE CUP-SEMI-FINALS.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CUP-SEMI-FINALS.</p>
        <p>*Wigan 3 3 15 Warrington *Oldham 4 6 26 Runcorn 1 1 7</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S GAME FIGHT AT WIGAN. The semi-finals for the Lancashire Cup were played on Saturday, and Warrington were drawn to play at Wigan against the home side, which would be rather a big order at any time. Warrington were handicapped by the absence of Thomas, O'Neill, Dowell, Treharne and Brooks, the first four being ineligible and the latter injured. Wigan were at full strength. The weather was fine, and the match attracted a large amount of I interest, about 1,000 prople attending from Warrington. There was a crowd of 19,000, and the receipts totalled £425, of which Warrington get half. The teams were:— Wigan—Sharrock, back; Ley tham, Jenkins, Todd and Miller, three-quarter backs ; Thomas and Weave, half backs ; Blears, Cheetham, Johnston, Silcock. Whittaker and Ramsdale, forwards. Warrington—Tilley, back ; Bradshaw, Taylor, Dickinson and Fish, three-quarter backs ; Lloyd and Jenkins, half backs ; A. Boardman, P. Boardman, Hewitt, Belton, Chesters and Naylor, forwards. I Referee, Mr. H. Farrar (Keighley) ; touch judges. Messrs. H. Critchey (Widnes), and S. Gregory (Salford). Warrington started against a light breeze, and immediately adopted aggressive tactics. Wirr'an were forced back to the line and smart handling by Warring- ton saw Taylor serve Bradshaw, with a nice pass, which was missed and a probable try lost. The visitors were not daunted, however, and fine forward play kept the home team penned in their quarters Lloyd got hold from the scrum, and beautiful passing by Jenkins, Taylor and Dickenson provided an opening for Fish. The wing man took the ball well and went over at the corner with a rare burst of speed. Sharrocks and the scorer were both laid out, but resumed. Dickenson made a good attempt at goal from the touch-line. Warrington kept up a hot attack and Wigan seemed unable to cope with the determined play of the Wirepullers. taylor broke through with the ball at his toes, and Bradshaw joined in. There was only the full back to plot when the wing man tried to pick up and knocked on. After this escape Wigan rallied to some purpose. The equalising points came half-way through the first " forty." Leytham crossing the line after Jenkins and Blears had handled the leather. Then Taylor was injured and had to retire. While Warrington were short-handed Leytham broke through the weakened defence again, and Sharrocks kicked a fine goal from near tench. Half-time came with Wigan leading by 8 points to 3. On resuming, Warrington again showed fine form, and were only kept out with difficulty. Thomas relieved with a brilliant run. Then Todd punted and tackled Dickenson near the line. The Warrington man played the ball with no supporters at hand, arid Gleave got an easy try near the posts, Sharrock gosling. The match seemed safe for Wigan now, though Fish scored a penalty goal. Fish changed the scene by intercepting a pass and racing to the home line when Sharrock brought him down by the foot. Before they could be cleared out, Jenkins dodged his way over near the posts, and Fish added a goal. The match was open again, and Bradshaw should have scared when put in possession near the line, but he failed to beat Sharrock. Wigan rushed to the other end, and after Tilley had pulled Ramsdale down under the posts, Slaarrocks kicked a penalty goal. Warrington tried hard to the end of a fine game. Final : Wigan, 3 goals 3 tries, 15 points ; Warrington, 2 goals 2 tries, 10 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS" A " v. WARRINGTON "A."—These teams tried conclusions at St. Helens on Saturday before a large attendance, the home side including the majority of the first team,who were without an engage- ment. It was expected that A. F. Kelly, the New Zealander, would play for the first time, but he was unable to turn out. 'Warrington started and a smart attack was repulsed by Manchester kicking into touch. Passing by the home men was spoiled by Manchester failing to take a pass from Hillen. Creevey was prominent with some fine kicking, and Flanagan received, but.offside spoiled the effort. Warrington put in a brisk attack and some scrambling play by tho home team forced Creevey to kick dead. Warrington pressed and were repeatedly dangerous near the St. Helens lino, but the defensive tactics of the home men withstood the attack. Half-time : St. Helens " A," nil ; Warrington " A," nil. Brisk attacks by the visitors characterized the reopening. Manchester was prominent with fine tackling. Warrington pressed, and some good work took place near the home line. \Var. rington got over but the point was not allowed. A free kick was awarded and Thomas failed at goal. Midfield play followed and for some time the game was evenly contested. Warrington attacked strongly in the closing stages and Haintith scored on the call of time, the vi si tors winning by one try (throe Points), to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>RUNCORN'S PROTEST DEFEATED.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN'S PROTEST DEFEATED.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Lancashire Senior Cup Committee was held in Manchester on Monday. Runcorn, who were defeated at Oldham in the semi-float of the Lancashire Cup last Saturday by 26 points to seven, laid a protest against the result of the game counting on the ground that a spectator came into the field of play and inter- fered with the Runcorn men tackling Dixon when he scored his try. Runcorn lodged their protest under by-law 23. The referee and touch judge both stated that a spectator did roll over the straw into the touch- in-goal area, Dixon, however, being over the lino when the incident named occurred. The Oldham Club took objection to the validity of the application, arguing that by-law 23 did not apply to the cise, which, it was contended, was covered by rule 13 oi the laws of the game, which gives the referee power to allow or disallow a try under the circumstances raised. The Committee decided that the protest was frivolous, and dismissed the same, ordering Runcorn's deposit to be forfeited, and them to pay the costs. The final tie in connection with the Lancashire Senior Cup between Oldham and Wigan was fixed to be played on the Broughton Rangers' ground on Saturday, December 13th.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE. PLAYER AND TOUCH JUDGE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Northern Rugby League Com- mittee, held at Huddersfield on Tuesday afternoon, the following League fixtures were rearranged :—Leigh v. Oldham from December sto December 9 ; Widnes v. Wigan from December 5 to February 11. Batley and Treherbert clubs were Oiled for late starts at Hull and Ebbw Vale respectively. R. Clampitt, Broughton Rangers, was suspended up to and including December 31 for insubordination and using bad language to one of the touch judges in the match Oldham v. Broughton Rangers on November f.tS, end for attempting to trip in the match Rochdale Hornets v. Broughton Rangers on December 5. The Broughton Rangers club preferred a charge against Mr. Jones, of Runcorn, of roughly Handling their player when charging him with insubordination and bad language. After hearing the evidence the committee decided to di4tuiss the case, and ordered Broughton Rangers Club to pay the expenses of Mr. Jones attend- ing the inquiry. APPRECIATION FOR WIGAN.</p>
        <p>The meeting of the Northern Union General Com- mittee, held at Huddersfield on Tuesday night, expressed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P S.A. SOCIETY. SuecesiFul—Conc ert.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P S.A. SOCIETY. SuecesiFul—Conc ert.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening a concert was given in Wycliffe Memorial Hall in aid of the funds of the above Society. Mr. B. Farrar presided and explained the objects of the concert, and expressed his pleasure at seeing so many present to help them in their good work. The programme included :—Pianoforte duet, Mr. Robert Lee and Mr. W. H. Payton ; pianoforte duet, Miss Jessie Farrar and Mr. Payton, selections from " The Mikado " ; songs, Mr. B. Hibbert, " Asleep In the deep " and "The Trumpeter " ; Mr. F. Field- , house, "Two eyes of grey,' "Marching along," " Glorious Devon," " In your dear eyes " ; Miss Emily Taylor, " The Waltz Song " and" April Song " ; Miss J. Armstrong, " The Raft "and " Going to Kildare. " Mr. George Smith gave several humorous songs and recitations, " Singers and Talkers," " Side Slips," "Troublesome dreams," "Devil My Care," all of which were greatly enjoyed. The concert throughout was of a first-class order and reflected great credit upon the artistes. The Chairman expressed the thanks of the gathering to all the artistes who had given so readily and willing their services for the help of the P.S.A., especially to Mr. W. 11. Payton, Mus. Bac, for securing the services of the artistes, and arranging the pro- gramme. Mr. Payton said it had been a great pleasure to him to arrange the concert, and if they wished at some future time he would be pleased to arrange another for them. OPEN MEETING. THE ARMENIAN ATROCITIES. There was a good attendance at an open meeting of the Society on Sunday afternoon when the Rev. H. H. Yazijian, spoke on the Martyr Church of Armenia. Councillor JOSEPH HAWTHORN, who presided, said their minds had recently been concentrated upon affairs in the Near East. The Balkan Crisis was practically an outcome of the rise to power of the Young Turks party which had precipitated events in the Balkans. Small states which had been under the suzerainty of the Turks were now shaking off their allegiance. Nt'hen one considered the past treatment of some of those States by the Turks it was not surpris- ing that there was a strong desire to break away from the Turkish yoke at the earliest opportunity. Followers of Mohammed themselves, the Turks had been cruel oppressors of Christians in their tributary States, and the meeting that afternoon would be reminded of their inhuman treatment of the Armenians—treatment which roused Mr. Gladstone from his retirement at Hawarden to pro- test against such iniquity. For years the Turks had been a painful blister on European politics. The treaty of Berlin in 1873 greatly reduced the area under Turkish rule, besides confirming the independence and extending the limits of several of the former tributary States. IVe were now witnessing a further lopping off of tributary States from the Turkish Empire, and those of us who loved freedom and right-dealing between nation and nation hoped the day was not far distant when theArrnenian people would be in a position to declare their independence, as the Bulgarians had recently done. There were many admirers of the Armenians in this country. They were a people with a history which went back to the dawn of civilzation in Europe. They were one of the first peoples to embrace Christianity. Their historic kingdom did not now politically exist, it having been shared amongst Turkey, Russia and Persia. The Turks had been cruel taskmasters. Thousands of Christian Armenians had been wantonly massacred and the end of the wicked was not yet. Their speaker had been a sufferer, from the cruelty of the Turks, and as the English people had always lent a willing ear to suffering and oppressed peoples, he (the Chairman) felt sure they would give Mr. Yazijian their hearty sympathy and support in the work he was doing., The Rev. H. H. YAZIJIAN, who is the representative of a very large and influential committee, whose head- quarters is Manchester,. spoke upon the society's work for the help and Lafety of the Armenian Christians in Asia Minor, deck upon the difficulties of the Christians under the Turkish rule, and also related some of the horrors perpetrated there some years ago. Mr. Yazijian speaks very broken English, yet can make himself understood fairly well. His daughter recited and sang in English and Armenian. A retiring collection was taken which realized RI es. 7d., and 58. 6d. for books showing the work in that oppressed country. The SECRETARY returned thanks for the two brethren who had received sympathetic letters the week before, and also proposed a vote of sympathy to the Gardiner families who were relatives of the man and his son. who walked into the canal near Rochdale on a foggy night and were drowned. One new member received a hearty welcome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>CHRISTMAS PARCELS.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS PARCELS.</p>
        <p>The London.and North-Western Railway Company have made complete arrangements for the collection, quick transit, and prompt delivery of Christmas parcels in all the principal towns on their system, and all parts .of Wales Scotland, and Ireland. Special "Parcel" trains will be run and through vans will also be attached to the principal passenger trains for the accommodation of the traffic, and additional delivery and collection services by parcel vans and carts between the Receiving Offices and the Railway Stations will be in operation during Christmas and the preceding week. Senders are advised that as an extra precaution, a duplicate address label should be enclosed in each parcel so that the name of the consignee may be ascertained in the event of the outside address becoming detached or defaced.</p>
      </div>
