        <p>ges Barrington, of London-road, Stockton Heath, an inspector of shops in the service of Messrs. llidgways, Limited, tea merchants, who was until March last a shop manager at Birkenhead, in the employ of the tame company. The alleged libel was in a letter 'which the defendant had sent to the managing director Ridgwaya Limited, charging the plaintiff with being drunk during business hours. The defence was privilege and justification. hen the jury had been absent fur close upon an hour, his Lordship asked whether the litigants would be willing to take the verdict of a majority. Counsel replied that the parties could 4ot agree to this course. Sr a y counsel retired to the judges's private room to discuss with him the possi- bility of an arrangement. When counsel returned into court, it was announced that the jury had agreed. They were brought back into the box, and their find. ing was received. They found for the plaintiff, with one farthing damages. Coleridge yesterday morning entered judgment for the plaintiff without costa. For all Chronic Chest Complaints, take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, is. lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
