        <p>• Victoria-road, Grappenhall. Residents in the neighbourhood of the above will be interested to learn that the architect's report on the road-making, kerbing, etc., was laid before the Runcorn Rural Council on Tuesday, and referred to a special committee. There has already been about 13 months' delay. Perhaps something will be done presently. PUBLIC B.DIEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK YOE BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Death of a Former Latchford Resident. The death is announced at 64, New-lane, Patricroft, of Mrs. Jeanette Warburton (widow af the late Mr. Richard Warburton, of Lymm) at the advanced age of SO years. Mrs. Warburton was the daughter of the late Mr. George Preston, a Lymm chemist, and for many years she resided at Latchford, where she was highly respected. The funeral took place on Tuesday at Barton. _ _</p>
        <p>Women's Liberal Association. At a committee meeting of the Association held on Friday last the following ladies were elected: Mrs. Parker, president in place of Mrs. Armitage resigned; vice-presidents, Mesdames Armitage, Guest,J.Magnn, Wallor ; Mrs. Withenshaw, Mrs. Pemberton,and Miss Short were added to the committee ; Mrs. Walker, treasurer ; Mrs. Osborne, secretary. Miss Dutton sent in her resignation as a member of the committee. LINSEED COMPOUND 'for Coughs and Colds. Of proven efficacy. 91d. &amp;19.11 d. Of Chemists only.—Advt. Warrington Women's Circle. Mrs. Clatworthy presided at the last meeting of the Circle, assisted by Mrs. Letheren and Mrs. Wynne Jones. A witty, shrewd, and eminently sensible address on "A Woman of Tact" (I. Sam. x-xv.) was given by Mrs. Madding (Padgate ) The triumph of Abigail over environment • the suitability of various Bible names; same clever Illustrations of the absurdi- ties of novelette reading, and wise advice for girls about to be marriel were given. Miss Blackstock sang two solos —" Sunshine and Rain " and "The Gates of the West," Miss Cox accompanying—On Thursday evening the members of Mrs. Moore's Bible Class gave a service of song entitled " Little Abe, the Bishop of Berry Brow." PUBLIC BENS FIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK F3ll BZI BOOTS IN LANCA.SHIRE.—Advt. The Infirmary Ball. We would remind our readers that the annual ball in aid of the funds of the Infirmary and Dispensary will he held in the Parr Hall, on December 29th. Bayley's band (augumented) has been engaged and Mr. Ralph Johnson will provide a special dancing floor. The committee earnestly solicit generous help to this effort. The enlargement of the Infirmary will, , it is estimated,entail an annual expenditure of E. 4,500.1 The assarred income is only £B5O. The annual ball is one of the means by which this expenditure is met, and it is hoped that the proceeds from the ball will be largely increased to help to meet this heavy expend- ture. Donations to the ball fund are accordingly earnestly solicited, and will be gratefully received by the honorary secretary (Captain G. R. Crosfield.) PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER THS CLOCK FORBEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Warrington Shopbreaker Sentenced. Page. At the Liverpool Assizes on Tuesday, before Mr. 2. Women's Chat and Fashions. Justice Channell.—Frederick Hallows, 24, joiner, Trades Council. Cumberl9d-street, Warrington, pleaded guilty to two Photographic Notes. charges'of shopbreaking at Warrington in November,' 3. Notes on Games and athletics. the premises which he entered being those of Messrs. Our Local Churches. J. and W. Collins, grocers of this town.—Mr. Madden Winwick Asylum Extension. prosecuted, and it apreared that on Use night of the , Retirement of Inspector Smith. 6th November the police were in the neighbourhood of Messrs. Collins' premises, and one of the officers 6. Board of Guardians. saw the figure of a man crouching down near the safe. I Stockton Heath Betting Raid The police entered and the man made his escape Children's Corner through the roof. He turned out to be the prisoner. The I 7. Football. block of buildings was surrounded and when prisoner Wycliffe P.S.A. attempted to get away he was pursued and after an 'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs &amp;Colds. Relieves exciting chase was captured. ! Several other cases of , Asthma and difficult breathing. 9icl., is. lid.—Advt. breaking into shops and offices in Warrington had Coming Events, been traced to him, and entrance to the premises wasl effected by boring a hole in the back door.—Prisoner The annual choir festival will be celebrated at ' I was sent to gaol for twelve calendar months with hard Bold-street Wesleyan-Church to-morrow (Sunday) when the preachers and subjects will be :—Morning labour. Hey. E. B. Keeling, "The Grace of Life"; evening, LINSEED COMPOUND' (Aniseed, Senega, Squill ' Bev. T. Miller, "Recreation and Religion." There aco.), for Coughs. Of Chemists only.—Advt. ° will he special anthems by th