      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>LORDS AND LICENSING. -- To-Night's Great Protest Meeting.</head>
        <p>LORDS AND LICENSING. -- To-Night's Great Protest Meeting.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Scheme for a New Bridge.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Bridges Sub-Committee was held ! at the Town Hall on Tuesday when Alderman Smethurst was re-appointed Chairman and the Mayor Alderman Forshaw) was elected to the vice-chair. It was resolved that a new steel bridge 80ft. wide be constructed in place of the existing Warrington Bridge and also that the erection of a footbridge at Rowley be at once proceeded with. These recommendations were submitted to a meet ing of the General Purposes Committee held after wards when it was decided that tenders be obtained for single elan steel bridges 6Oft. and 801 t. wide respectively. When a tender has been accepted it will probably be necessary to apply to Parliament for permission to include the scheme in a Bill, as longer borrowing powers would be obtained in that way. The recommendation with regard to Howley foot- bridge was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 12, 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>NAVAL ABSENTEES AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Francis Ruffen, a Widnes youth, and Joseph Seeley, a native of London, were apprehended at Widnes on Sunday afternoon for being absentees from his Majesty's ship Africa, stationed at Portland. A few days ago they were granted twenty-four hours' leave, when they took train from Weymouth to Liverpool, and arrived in Widnes on Thursday, stranded. A warrant was issued, and Police-constable Scott arrested them on Sunday. As the pass in their possession was not in order they were sent to the Police Station and conveyed to their ship by Police-constables Scott and Cleal on Monday morning. I WIDNES MAN CUT TO PIECES.</p>
        <p>Early on Monday the body of James Cook, aged between forty and fifty, a boilermaker's labourer, resid- ing at '2O, Frank-street, Widnes was picked up on the Cheshire Lines Railway near Widnes, Station. The body was in a frightful state of mutilation. The head and arms were severed. Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy-coroner, held the inquest at I the Widnes Police Station on Wednesday. From the evidence it transpired that the deceased had been out of work for ten days, and was under the doctor suffer- ing from catarrh of the stomach. He left home on Sunday morning, and was not seen alive again. The deceased had a sister residing in Upton, and by crossing the line it afforded a short cut to her house.—Mr. R. Nutter, station-master, who found the body, said it was lying straght across the 6ft. way, whilst the head,which was cleanly severed, lay in the 4ft. way.—Police- Constable Yates gave it as his opinion that the man muss have got down on his stomach, because of the position in which he found the arms.—A verdict of Found dead on the line " was returned, but there was not sufficient evidence to show how it came about. WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL. ST. BEDE'S PROPOSAL. SCHEME ADOPTED.</p>
        <p>There was full complement of Councillors present on Tuesday at the meeting of the Widnes Town Council, when the adjourned debate on the St. Bede's-road scheme was resumed. The scheme as suggested by the Special Committee, is for the making of a road 1,100 yards long from Vic;oria-rosd to Birchfield-road. Land has been acquired from the Leigh Estate and from Mr. G. W. Knowles. The committee have acquired land from the latter for the sum of £2,500, and, with an interchange of a portion of this with the Leigh Estate trustees who are prepared to pay the Corporation £l,BOO towards the cost, the Corporation have the necessary land to go on with the road. The land lies just behind the populous Simms , Cross district and the inhabitants anticipating a diver- t sion of traffic throuth that quarter, opposed the scheme. I Mr. Welding, who was the first to rise in opposition, said the public had got the ear of the promoters' side only. The report had been very astutely drawn up. If the scheme was adopted it would only benefit a few, and he did not see why they should spend £5,000 to 'suit them. lAlderman Neil said there was some kind of mis- understanding. If they were hived in a congested t district the road would be required, but as they were I not it was not necessary. If Deacon-road and Frederic- street had been made, nothing would have been said of St. Bede'-road. As a property owner in Simms Cross Ward, be did not think it fair to divert the present road, by going on with the proposed one. He was only voicing tbe sentiments of dozens of shopkeepers who were opposed to it. Mr. G. Davies said he had no wish to help the Leigh Estate, but one had to look at the town as a whole. The , making of the road would tend towards the develop- ment of the town, and whilst he had large interests in Simms Cross, he sincerely hoped they would make the town more compact by supporting the committee. Councillor Clarke contended that the road was not required at the present time. The scheme was a paper one only, and would mean an increase on the rates. Alderman Owens, referring to the excellent terms offered to the committee, hoped the matter would have the committee's earnest consideration. It was one of the finest schemes brought before the Council, and they would be very foolish if they rejected it. Councillor Lewis spoke in support of the scheme, while Alderman Quinn objected, on the grounds that the cost of the making of the road, as presented, was not accurate. Alderman Timms reviewed the whole scheme, and expressed surprise at the attitude of several of the members. Upon the vote being taken, 16 voted for and 8 against. Alderman Waring, in moving the adoption of the Gas and Water Committees' minutes, referred to the efficiency test of the new Henry Sutton Timmis engine, which has been put down at Stocks Well at a cost of , 216,000. He said the contract speed was to deliver 3,000,000 gallons per day into the reservoir at the rate of 13A strokes per minute. The speed during the test I was 141 strokes per minutes, at the rate of nearly ; 4,000,000 gallons, or an average of 30 per cent. over the i contract speed. The total sum expended on the exten- sion, which included £320 efficiency premium paid to ; the makers was a little over £16,000. The saving on I the coal bill alone will provide a sum sufficient to pay •, interest and sinking fund on the total capital expendi- -1 ture, and leave a surplus of £4OO. Last year the total I water pumped was 161,000,000 gallons, but now, with ' the new Henry Sutton engine, it is possible to pump 2,000,000,000 gallons and of the value of this extra supply of water at 3d. par 1,000, or £lO,OOO per annum, two-thirds would be profit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>KILLED BY A CAPSTAN. An inquest was held at the Earlestown Town Hall, on Monday into the death of Alfred Isherwood, aged thirty-nine, of Wood-street, Earlestown, who became i entangled n the rope of a capstan while following his employment as a labourer at the London and North- Western Railway Waggon Works at Earlestown' on Thursday morning, and was instantly killed. The Coroner (Mr. S. Brighouse) remarked that the man bad no right, according to the company's rules, to start the capstan working unless authorised by the capstan-man, but no doubt he was prompted by a desire to do the best for his employers--A verdict of " Accidental death" was returned, no blame being attached to turf one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>POLES AT VARIANCE,</head>
        <p>POLES AT VARIANCE,</p>
        <p>William X°lita, a Pole, living in Athol-street,Earles- town, was charged at St. Helens, on Monday, with wounding his brother Joseph by stabbing him at his house in Bourne-street, Peasley Cross, on Saturday night.—The Chief Constable said all the parties were Poles, and only partially understood English, but the case was a serious one of stabbing. About nine o'clock on Saturday night William arrived at Joseph's house, and, after they had had some drink, a quarrel arose., William attacked his brother with a glass, and after- wards with a knife. He was put out of the house by some other men, but afterwards Joseph let him in again, whereupon accused pulled out a knife, and struck his brother several blows on the head. He ran out into Peasley Cross-lane, and gave information to Inspector Jackson. Dr. Davies was afterwards called to the man, and found a large circular wound behind the man's ear. He had four other serious wounds.—Joseph said he was sorry to have to prosecute his brother, but when he aot drunk he was not safe.—William was sent to gaol for two months, the Bench remarking that he might easily have killed his brother. NEWTON URBAN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The ordinary meeting of the Newton Urban Council was held on Monday, at the Town Hall, Earlestown. Mr. W. Thompson presided, and there were also present: —Messrs. C. B. F. Borron, E. T. Evans, G. F. Clarke, W. Collingwood, J. Naylor, T. Ritchie, M. Seton, A. Tunstall, J. Higham, L. E. Pilkington, C. Cole (clerk), I A. Bowes (surveyor), and R. T. Surteee (water engineer). I The Clerk was instructed to again apply to the Local I Government Board for their sanction to the borrowing of the money required for erecting the proposed I technical school.—The Gas Engineer reported at a I I meeting of the Gas Committee on November 18th, that as requested, he had examined Golborne Dale-road with I a view to its lighting, and that be estimated the cost I of laying a main from the Bull's Head Inn to the Bull I cottages, and to provide one lamp at the cottages, would be £3o,—lt was resolved that the question of the light- ing of Golborne Dale road be deferred until the next I meeting, to enable the Gas Engineer to ascertain whether there is any probability of gas being required for the cottages there. The minutes were confirmed.— At a meeting of the Finance Committee on November 16th, the report of the assistant district auditor of his recent audit of the accounts of the Council for the year ended the 31st March last was read, from which it appeared that he had pleasure in stating that the accounts were well kept and that he did not find it , necessary to make any surcharge or disallowance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>FALSE PRETENCES AT ST. HELENS. At Liverpool Assizes on Monday, James Bibby (58), labourer, for having, at St. Helens, obtained ss. by false pretences, was sent to prison for twelve months with hard labour.</p>
        <p>ATTEMPTED SUICIDE AT ST. HELENS. Ellen Potter, a domestic servant, of Bruce-street, was charged at St. Helens on Monday with attempted suicide. The Chief Constable said that on Saturday evening the young woman tried to drown herself in the Leg of Mutton Dam at Taylor Park. Shortly after six o'clock Thomas Stinton, collier, of Doulton-street, was going along a road near the park with a companion, when he heard screams. He ran to the place and climbed over the gate to the water. He saw the young woman's cape by the side of the water, and she was face downwards struggling. Fortunately Stinton could swim, and he threw off his coat and swam to her. The water at this place was six or seven feet deep.,Stinton got the young woman to the surface and brought her to the side. She was then thoroughly exhausted, and he applied artificial respiration. Afterwards the police removed her to hospital. From what he could make not the girl was depressed. The father and aunt of the girl appeared and said they would look after her, and on this understanding she as discharged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
