      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>Old Age Pensions.</p>
        <p>Sir,—No doubt more will be heard about the old age pension on and after New Year's Day, when it has become an accomplished fact. It will be a boon impossible to estimate; relieving the aged people of many anxieties as to their future. But is anything going to be done for those persons over 70 sears who are debarred from the pensions because they have had the misfortune to apply for a little Parish-relief during the last year. Who is more deserving? Who has a greater claim? than those whom the Relieving Officer has already granted 2s. or 2s. 6d. a week; and poverty is their only disgrace. In several towns the Guardians of the Poor have already expressed theirintention of increasing the out-relief, so that it may compare equitably in snob cases. Will our Guardians rise to the occasion and do likewise ?—Yours truly, P. MORRALL. 63, Orford-avenue, Warrington.</p>
        <p>An Appeal for Poor Children. Sir,—Will you kindly allow me to lay before the readers of your valuable paper a few facts as to the severe distress which exists amongst the poor peoples Being connected with the Ragged School, it is part of our duty to visit these cases and we find that homes are without fire, children half-naked and crying for bread and destitute of all necessities of life. We are going to open the soup kitchen again which did such valuable work three years ago, and for this purpose we shall require anything in the way of vegetables, meat, or bread, as we purpose distributing 60 gallons of soup and 300 pounds of bread daily. I strongly appeal to all to help us. Our school in Bank-street will be open to receive goods each day, and we shall be pleased to send our collecting cart to any part of the town on receipt of news. Any cast-off clothing would be useful and will be given to those whom we know require it. If there are any person desirous of helping us either by collecting the articles or making the soup, or by distributing the tickets, we shall be glad of their services.—Yours Stc., F. OWEN, 425, Wellfield-street, 10th Dec., 1908.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>VVesleyans and Dr. Clifford.</head>
        <p>VVesleyans and Dr. Clifford.</p>
        <p>- Sir,—ln this month's issue of the " Warrington 1 Circuit Wesleyan Methodist Church Record" I find in the local Editorial notes, the following insulting refer- ence to Dr. Clifford ; and I am sure every fair-minded " Methodist "will deprecate its insertion. Here is the precious paragraph : "We regret that Dr. Clifford recognises the present situation as an opportunity for fresh experi- ments in political anarchy. This time he calls for passive resistance on the part of the payers of income tax. As long as Free Church leaders show an irresponsibility of speech, which we have hitherto associated with Mr. Victor Grayson and ennobled licensed victuallers, we sincerely trust that the Methodist public will not look for guid- ance in that direction." To associate Dr. Clifford with licensed victuallers and with Mr. 'Victor Grayson in this fashion is unworthy of any Christian, and is surely "hitting below the belt." Whether we see eye to eye with Dr. Clifford or not—l do not in all things—every honest man must acknowledge and admire his exalted character, his transparent honesty, his absolute integrity, and his charming personality. Those who know him best know that while immoveable as a rock where root prin- ciples are involved, no one is more open to hear both sides of a question, or to compromise where just compromise is possible. In regard to the last Education Bill he was one of those openly favour- able to conciliation and compromise, and no-one with the least sense of fsirness or of the fitness of things could justly write of him as above, or compare him with Victor Grayson or with drink sellers. I have often wished we had a few men of the stamp of Dr. Clifford in the Wesleyan Church. In these days it needs them as much as,unfortunately, it lacks them. We have one or two good men in some fields; men such as Scott Lidgett and Moulton. On the whole, however, they are much of a muchness, and that not much.—Yours, December llth, 1903.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Guardians' " Nemo Fund."</head>
        <p>The Guardians' " Nemo Fund."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l see the " Nemo Fund "was the subject of a lengthy and interesting discussion at the last meeting of the Board. You said in your "leader," and rightly so, that " some of the ingredients which go to fill up the cup are not without interest and merit some attention," and again, " there is evidently considerable justification for a large proportion of payments, but which might not have passed the fastidious eye of a Government Auditor." That is evidently so, and when made known to the public will not pass the fastidious eye of the ratepayer. In the first place, the general public did not know that there was such a fund in existence, or on what the money was spent, or by wham, or who controlled the Fund, and the latter is an important matter to know, for we are told that it was " controlled " by only a small "clique "of the Board, many of the members of the Board knowing nothing of the Fund. The curtain has been lifted just a little bit, and we are allowed to see the vanishing proportions of an operation of which the public ought to have fell particulars. It is perfectly clear that the statements PADGAT E - at the Board meeting are not "the whole truth, and nothing but the truth." Had I not run accidentally against " Copy of Particulars"(?) supplied to members of the "A VISIT TO THE FRANCO-BRITISH Board, who have maintained a sinister silence on this EXHIBITION." matter, I should have known no more than the general On Thursday a lecture entitled "A Visit to the public, but now, I have my thoughts, and one is, that Franco-British Exhibition" was given by Dr. Bennett, the Local Government Board ought to be called upon in the Wesleyan schoolroom at Padgate. Mr. H.S. Page to make an inquiry into the entire disposal of the presided. la opening the proceedings he (Mr. Page) Fund. The Fund and its disbursement has been in gave some interesting facts relating to the exhibition, existence for more than 20 years, so says Mr. A. J. especially mentioning the wonderful collection of Jolley. It has been made up of "drafts "from Bank 3,000 pictures in the Palace of Arm, which Interest on the General Account, so says the "bill of of course, would naturally appeal to him. He particulars." But this interest never ought to have regretted the weather was so unpropitious, but been withdrawn from the General Account. The was glad so many had weathered the storm. amount so withdrawn during the past five years , Dr. Bennett commenced his lecture by exhibiting and amounts to £419 6s. 9d. which with a balance when they describing some magnificent limelight views of the began the last five years (£137 10s. 3d.) makes £556 17s. great White City with its flue architecture and artistic This amount has been placed to a " Private Fund c beauty,iucluding the Court of Honour with its wonder- Account " and this Account has not been subjected to ful cascade (by day and night) ; court of arts, palace official audit since 1832, for the Clerk says "This of women's work, Royal pavilion, palace of fine arts, Account has not been brought before the Auditor— palace of French and British industries and applied not since 1832." At that period, the Auditor reported arts, restaurant paillard, imperial sports club, palace the Fund to be "illegal" but a year ago, with the of music. The views of the Colonial department were advent of a new clerk, the account was again sobject excellent. The lecturer stated that the Canadian to the view of the Auditor, who promptly called the ' pavilion waa one of the largest and most attractive Board's attention to the illegal nature of the Fund, 1 portions of the Exhibition ; it was replete with points land it was considered prudent to dispense with it, of interest to all visitors, the interior decorations to save further trouble. So much then for the stater consisting of red ground relieved by an extraordinary meat that some of the items appear to be legitimate.l decorative scheme worked ont in heads of wheat and And now as to 