      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. " The Love of the Princess."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. " The Love of the Princess."</p>
        <p>A romantic play with the above title, from the pen ! of C. Watson-Mill, is the attractia's at the Royal Court ' Theatre this week, and has afforded considerable pleasure to large audiences. It is a story of the inner life of modern Courts, in which the leading figures are Prince Conrad Ramona of Uldersbury,and Princess Idalia,heiress to the throne of Narava. The Prince is one of those courageous, high-spirited characters which always have a certain amount of glamour when portrayed on the stage, but unfortunately he is a victim to the gambling habit, which has for genera- tions been the besetting failing of the Uldersbur,,ris. He falls into the hands of sharpers, and when the play opens he has just sacrificed the ancestral homes at the gaming table. Naturally he is denounced as a worthless profligate, and the Prince Regent gives him the alternatives of making a speedy exit from this world by means of a revolver shot or of being publicly dis- graced before his regiment. The young man is about to take the former course when he is prevented by the timely arrival of Princess Idalia for whom he has long cherished a secret affection but who is generally regarded as the bride-elect of the King of Ilarnia. She dissuades him from his rash purpose and, encouraged by her trust in his honour, he resolves to battle with life anew and redeem his good name. How he succeeds, in spite of all manner of con- spiracies to still further entangle him, is capitally told in the play. His enemies are brought low and he achieves the desire which has been his guiding star throughout by, marrying the Princess. Mr. Wile ni Coleman is a dashing prince and thoroughly enters into the spirit of a part for which by voice and bearing he is eminently fitted. Miss Masie Hanbury captivates the audience as the charming Princess Idalia, for she is natural yet most effective in her acting. Mr. Ernest E. Imeson does well in the unenviable part of Count Nicholas Danillo, the traitor, and Ur. G. EL Dobell is sufficiently grim as King Josef. Mr. Francis Serle presents a clever study of Duke Searle, a wealthy parvenue, who is outwardly childlike and bland yet capable of subtle scheming. Mr. Beckett Bould as Lieutenant Orlans and Mr. Graham Winsford as Lieutenant Zaroff are a pair of gallant officers who quickly gain the favour of the audience. Miss Nellie Garside is most successful as Lady Irene Ramona, who has an embarrassing choice of suitors, and Miss Helen Elmer is well placed as Constantia, sister of mercy and Ilarnia's Queen. Mr. Edgar Morgan is ' responsible for much merriment as the bibulous yet , faithful servant Boris. The scenery and dresses are on a gorgeous scale and excite general admiration. The theatre orchestra has now been increased and the ; improvetnent is much appreciated by the patrons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Next week Mr. Geoffrey Fulton's Company will produce the modern drama entitled "A. girl redeemed from Sin," by David Musketry, author of " The poor must live" and "The dear homeland." The play tells the story of a mill lass and her life in London. Miss Olive Fait Hi will appear as Nancy Lee and Mr Jo. Markhouse as Will May.</p>
        <p>DRAMATIC SOCIETY'S PERFORIIANCES. The Warrington Dramatic Society announce that they will present the farcial comedy " The Lady of Ostend," at the Royal Court Theatre during the week commencing Bth February, I9og.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>RE-OPIINING OF THE WEsLEYIN SUNDAY SCHOOL.— On Saturday evening the formal opening of the above schools was performed. Mr. John Atherton occupied the chair, and in his remarks said how pleased he was to see the school finished. He well remembered the old chapel and Sunday school in Stocks-lane.—Mr. Thomas Bell read the balance sheet for the new organ in the chapel, and enlargement and alterations of the school, whioh showed that the total cost of same was £1,063, and that the sum of £1,013 3s. 3d. had been received towards that sun:l.—Addresses were given by the follow- ing :—Rev. John Gunnell, Rev. A. C. Parker, Rev. F. W. Remick, Rev. E. J. Gawne.—A very pleasing duty was performed by Mrs. James Dodds, who presented the Rev. E. J. Gawne with three beautiful pictures, on behalf of those who had been associated with the rev. gentleman while he was minister at Penketh.—Mr. C. Parke: aiso presented the Lite earetskor •-•1 the chapel and schools (Mr. W. Moores) with a splendid time- piece. He said Mr. l'iloopes had been an excellent care- taker for 21 years. The choir effectively rendered several choral items.—On the proposition of Mr. J. G. Withenshnw, seconded by Mr. J. Carman, a vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman and speakers.— Mr. G. Weaver accompanied on the piano. MINSTREL Coxossr.—A minstrel concert was given In the Wesleyan schoolroom on Monday by the Merry Minstrel Troupe. These merry,dark gentlemen, though only amateurs, rightly deserve to be warmly praised. A large audience assembled to give them a welcome as this was the first concert in the schools since the altera- tions. The performance was opened with a good chorus. There were some very good songs,both sentimental and humorous, which were rendered in grand style,and were warmly applauded. The jokes and stump speech were also well received. Carl Vernon gave an excellent ventriloquist show. In the second half of the concert he practically held the audienoe spell-bound with his imitations and sleight-of-hand tricks. After this there was a screaming farce entitled "Fun in a registry office." The actors acquitted themselves in grand style, both gentlemen's and ladies' parts:being taken by the gentlemen. Mrs. Stevenson officiated at the piano In a very capable manner, and deep regret is felt by the members and all who knew them that Mr. and Mrs. Stevenson have now left the district, as they were always ready to help any good Cause. Amongst those , who took part in the entertainment were Messrs. W. Foden, Critchley, Tucker, Richardson, J. Hughes, J. J. Peden, H. Shaw, A. T. Clay, M. Budden, and A. Dean.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>FOOTBALL FIXTURES AND RESULTS.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL FIXTURES AND RESULTS.</p>
        <p>PRESS Ass OCIATION'.— Special Wire,</p>
        <p>The Primate Minister was to-night the guest of his supporters in the House of Commons, who entertained the Right Hon. gentleman at the National Liberal Club as an acknowledment of his conduct of the Licensing Bill.</p>
        <p>Ma. A3-QUITII, responding to the toast of his health, said they were met, not in a penitential mood, not to acknowledge their unprofitableness, or to take upon their ups language either of remorse or apology. They were met to celebrate a failure, but a failure of pregnant meaning. Before a word had _been uttered in the House of Lords on the Licensing Bill, its fate was sealed. The question he wished to put to them and to his fellow Liberals outside was: Is this state of things to continue? We say it must be brought to an end (loud and continued cheering). Gentlemen, I invite the Liberal party to-night to treat the veto of the House of Lords as the dominating issue in politics —(renewed demonstrations, members rising to their feet)—the dominating issue because in the long run it absorbed and overshadowed every other.</p>
        <p>He would be asked to-morrow morning why did he not advise His majesty to dissolve Parliament. That would be to admit a new claim on the part of the House of Lords. He ventured to make two assertions: first, that such claim was historically untenable, the second was that from a practical point of view it was immeasurably absurd. (Loud cheers). He did not mean to create such a precedent, (renewed cheers). Under such terms the whole life of a Liberal Government would be spent session after session in a series of general elections. That was an unfounded and an inadmissable claim and the Government would adv ise the King to dissolve not at a time prescribed in its own caprice by an irresponsible assembly, but at a time neither sooner or later, which they with the con - fidence of their elected representative supporters in their unfettered discretion deemed to be right. Furthermore, that the next session should have a colourless programme would be for the Liberal Party I a confession of humiliation. (Cheers).</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that a short time ago the mportant question of whether dividend on purchases rem the Co-operative Society is to be regardod as noome came before the Warrington Pensions Com- mittee. The pensions °facers took the view that the dividend was income but the committee were of the contrary opinion and the point was referred to the Local Government Board for deoision. That authority's finding has now been forwarded and it is in favour of the committee's view that Co-operative dividend is not Income for the purposes of the Old Age Pensions Act. A meeting of the Pensions Committee was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday, under the presidency of the Mayor, when 62 new claims were considered, making a total of 414 to date. The following grants were made : —4B pensions of ss. per week ; one of 4s. ; two of 35.: two of 25.; one of Is. Seven claims were sojourned and one was disallowed. The committee also had before them 45 claims adjourned from previous meet- ings and the claimants were given the opportunity of appearing and stating the grounds of their applications. The pensions now granted by the Warrington Com- mittee will total about £lOO per week. The case mentioned at the meeting of the Board of Guardians by Coancillor James Evans in which a pension was claimed for a person who had been trans- ferred from the Warrington Infirmary to the Union Hospital, was discussed and the Committee decided to grant a pension.</p>
        <p>(NORTIIERN UNION LEA.GUE.)</p>
