        <p>!The second of a series of boxing competitions in Connection with the Bth and 40th Regimental Districts i was held on Thursday night, in the gymnasium, Orford. I Barracks, Warrington. There was an excellent attend- ! ance of officers and townspeople, including the Mayor (Alderman Forshaw). In the various competitions , some fine boxing was witnessed. The following are the I reselts :—Light weights (10st. and under) : Corporal Gotenby, South Lancashire Regiment, defeated Private , Brennon, 3rd South Lancashire. The club light ? ~, , weights was won by Lance-Corporal Dowell, musical Sarvice at St. Barnabas' Church. 3rd King's, who gained the ver Bet against Private , A musical service was held at St. Barnabas' Church Dixon, 3rd South Lancashire. The latter was forced on Sunday afternoon in aid of the choir and organ to retire with a damaged thumb. In the club feather- funds. A large congregation assembled and listened , weights, Private Collins, 3rd South Lancashire Regi- ment, won on points over his opponent, Privatewith great appreciation to the following selections by , club Malone, the Apollo Prize Male Voice Choir :—" Martyrs; of lof the 4th King's. Aneight-round contest for the . the Arena," "Son of God," "God save the people" and Corporal Vaughan, Manchester Regiment, A I purse between Gunner Budgeon, R.P.A., Prestons, atonotn. "Father all glorious," all of which were most expres- under-Lyne, proved an easy affair for Ilndge?n, wa I h ,r) sively given. Mr. Harry Berrey conducted. Mr. W. ' knocked his man out in the second round. Local interest Tynan sang "The Man of Sorrows " and Mr. Edgar was centred in the fifteen-round contest between G. Noden gave " Nearer my God to Thee." The duets Warren and J. England. The combatants were very "Love Divine" and "My song shall be always of Thy . ' evenly matched, and at the end of the final round the Mercy," were interpreted by Miss P. Bennett and Mr. referee, Mr. F. Tollitt, of London, declared a draw. A. Gree