      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT. BILL FOR THE TAXATION OF LAND YALUES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT. BILL FOR THE TAXATION OF LAND YALUES.</p>
        <p>The Parliamentary Debating Society took possession of its new quarters—the Assembly Room, Conservative Club—on 'Thursday evening, and discussed the last measure to be introduced by the present Liberal administration—a Bill" to provide an equitable basis for the taxation and exchange of laud and other like ' property to meet the requirements of Imperial and Local Government and well-being." The session now drawing to a close can be regarded as a success for although the attendances have not always been large, the Society is undoubtedly fulfilling its chief purpose, which is to train its members in the art of public speaking. Almost every sitting has been marked by several maiden efforts and as the inexperi- eneed aspirant to oratorical honours always meets with an encouraging reception from all parts of the House, he is emboldened to repeat the experiment and thus gains in confidence and in ability to express his thoughts freely. The most remarkable feature of Thursday evening's debate was that no serious opposition could be found to the Government Bill. Conservative leaders bestowed on it their blessing as far as its general prinoiples were concerned, and Socialists joyfully welcomed it. The prospects are that the Bill will be passed into law with- out a division, for no one has yet ventured to move its rejection. Mr. W. EL Susiteurme (Secretary for Scotland) introduced the Bill, and in the course of a lucid explana- tion, said the Government wished this to be the crown- ing measure of a not unfruitful term of office. The people would not get the advantage of any measure of , reform until such a Bill had been passed, because under ' the present conditions the landlord reaped the greater part of the benefit. He claimed that the question was a national and not a party one. Even under the last Con- servative Government a majority of the House decided in favour of some form of taxation of land values. It was no good dealing with housing, small holdings, unemployment or anything else until they had settled the land question. Why did the Government wish to tax land values? Because land values were made , altogether by the industry of the people, and were reaped by those who did not take any large active part in that industry. In London alone 20 million pounds per year were drawn in ground rents by people who did not help London or the country in any way so far as their industry was con- cerned and for which they paid very little in taxation. Mr. Sherburn gave several telling instances of the enormous profits which accrued to the landlords through the enterprise and growth of the community. Under the new Bill the value placed upon the land by the landlord or his agent was to be the value for rating, or for purchasing when the representatives of the people proved that it was necessary for the good of the com- munity. The landlord would not then be liable to value the land too highly because he would want to keep the rates low and he would not value it too modestly for fear someone would want to buy it. (La ughter and applause.) Mr. P. O'Bnixs (St. Pancras) said the Bill was particularly non-ooatroversial because the value of the land in any town was not entirely created by the members of one political party. At present the workers of all kinds were taxed for the benefit of the few who acquired land by means which there was no necessity to go into. (Laughter.) They did not want to rob the landlords but to tax the land up to its full value. (Applause). Mr. W. Cimains (S. Birmingham) said he did not feel very much inclined to criticise the Bill. He quite agreed there was no necessity to make it a party question, because they all believed in justice to every- one. He congratulated the Secretary for Scotland on the moderation with which he had introduced the measure. On the whole he considered it was a very sensible Bill. The noblemen who held so much land ought certainly to be compelled to contribute very largely to the Exchequer, and he could hardly imagine that they would refuse. (Ministerial " 0 h's "). He did not hold with the idea that all noblemen had come into their property by stealing it or by unjust means. If a nobleman's ancestors acquired land improperly, we had no right to charge the present holder with dis- honesty. On the whole he very much approved of the Bill. Mr. JAS. BALL (Wolverhampton) slid the Bill was calculated to appeal to the sense of fairness and justice on all sides of the House. In the first place land was granted to the forefathers of the noblemen for the services they had to render in times of war,and so long as they fulfilled the duty to the State of providing equipment, men and provisions for the various campaigns they were entitled to hold the land in fee. This service was, however, discontinued and the landed gentry impOsed a tax on their own land which was supposed to be in place of the military help. The land- owners were bound to see that whereas the tax in times gone by was adequate in proportion to the general taxa- tion of the Kingdom it was not to-day, but as they had not made any offer to revise the tax it devolved upon others who wanted something like fairness and justice to see that they were compelled. As showing how the value of land was enhanced by industrial developments, Mr. Ball said a firm of manufacturers in Warrington took some land which was fetchieg £BO per year as agricultural land, and they were now being mulcted in the sum of about Z3OO per year. 100 only t.tithn. 1.0 hos; to off,r against the Bill was that the amount received from the taxation of land values ought to be allocated to the towns by the efforts of whose tradesmen, workers and manufacturers, the value of the land had been increased. Warrington Bridge would not be the disgrace it was to-day if we only had a new source of revenue to tap. Although the Socialists would desire a larger and bolder scheme, they welcomed the Bill as a step in the right direction. Mr. C. RoonY (E. Mayo) spoke on the injustice of the Irish land system and caused some amusement by remarking that Ireland was driving the capitalist away from that country and when Englishmen saw the success of the policy they would follow suit. (Laughter.) HAIXTITORNE (E. Belfast) said the term "holder" In the Bill was ambiguous. Oaght they not to sub- stitute " owner"? Mr. SHERBITIai : There is no suc'a thing as a land-</p>
        <p>" owner." Mr. ll.tvimfonNE said the question was who would pay the tax in the end? He was afraid the man who received the small wage would suffer. The Secretary for Scotland had said much about the growth of the value of land but he omitted to say anything about depreciation. ' Mr. CARR (E. Leeds) supported the Bill as a means of taxing unearned increment. He denounced indirect taxation as a curse and said direct taxation would do a great deal to dispel the apathy of people towards the well-being of themselves and their fellowmen. The Bill was the sanest measure yet put forward to deal with unemployment. Mr. W. H. STANSFIELD (Dublin University) said he could not congratulate the Secretary for Scotland on introducing the Bill because it was one with which eyerybody could agree. He would have preferred a Bill which they might have been able to light about. (Laughter.) The Liberals had been stumping the country about the [loose of Lords and if they had brought in a measure to abolish that Elouse,they would have had something to keep them warm. (Laughter.) However, they preferred to have a back-handed slap at the Lords though the present Bill. The Bill was not a new source of revenue but an old source revived. He quite agreed that the landlords ought to pay their share to the revenue of the country because they had ceased to perform the obligations for which the land was originally granted. Mr. Stanslield suggested that a tax of d. on the capital value would press somewhat heavily if the landlords had to pay income tax as well. On the whole, however, he approved of the Bill. Mr. JOHNSTONE (Dumfries) said all the solid reason- ing support of the Bill had come from the progressive quarter of the House and the unreasoning support from the Tories. (Laughter.) It was obvious that there must be a very glaring injustice if the Tory party sup- ported the measure even in a slight degree. The debate was then adjourned until next Thursday evenino. On Wednesday evening next a smoking concert will be held at the Lion Elute!. BOXING AT THE BARRACKS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-12_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
