        <p>A BREEZE IN COURT. Lively Passage between Solicitor and Magistrate. A lively scene oocurred at the Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, during the hearing of a case in which Mary Powell, licensee of the Wheat Sheaf Inn, Antrobus ,was summoned for permitting drunkenness on her licensed premises on November 29th. Mr. Giles, Chester, prosecuted on behalf of the police, and Mr. Browne, Warrington, appeared for the defendant. ihe magistrates on the bench were Messrs. J. Charlton Parr (chairman), George Dakin, H. Roberts, L. Green- ing and C. T. Whitley. A profcocional acrobat named Benjamin Rdstage,who had signed a statement to the effect that a man named Donkey concerned in the case was drunk but who now said was of a different opinion,was giving evidence when Mr. Roberts complained that Mr. Browne was putting words into the witness's mouth. Mr. Browne: The witness is swearing that the officers went into the house three times. Mr. Roberts: This man will swear anything. Mr. Browne : A man who says that has no right to sit on the bench. Mr. Roberts : The witness has signed a written statement, which he gave to the police, and he now denies what he said then. Mr. Browne: lam only in the habit of answering the Chairman and I will answer nobody else. !qr. Roberts : You can please yourself whether you answer me or not. lam not bound by your commands. I have a duty to perform as a magistrate just as you have as an advocate, and I shall perform it. The case for the prosecution was that on the day in question Sergeant Bostock and Constable Dutton visited the Wheat Sheaf Inn and in the taproom found eight men and one woman. A man named Joseph Hankey was amongst the company, and, in the opinion of the officers, he was drunk. Hankey was taken in a float to the Police Station. He was totally incapable of looking after himself. The defence was that defendant had no knowledge that llankey was in the house. She had nothing to do with the management-of the business on the day in question, but left it to her nephew, Mr. Holt. After hearing evidence, the Bench rot irecl,and on their [ return their Chairman said defendant would be fined 20s. and costs. The advocate's fee was also allowed. Joseph lianhey, who resides at Crowley, was then -charged u ith having I).3en drunk on the licensed premises of the Wheat Sheaf Inn on November 28th, and was fined 10s.</p>
