        <p>Warrington Juryman's Complaint. At the conclusion of an inquest held at the War- rington Police Station, on Monday, one of the jurymen made a complaint to the coroner with regard to the treatment of jurymen and the inconvenience caused by having to walk long distances in order to view bodies. He thought that a conveyance ought to b e supplied.—The Coroner (Mr. Brighouse) was quite in sympathy with the complaint. Lfe thought it quite sufficient for a juryman to give his time without being called upon to walk outrageously long distances in order to view bodies. He stated that in Liverpool and Southport, cabs and wagonettes are supplied in order to take jurymen to and from the viewing of bodies. The matter did not rest with him. He suggested they should put themselves in direct communication with the Chief Constable, and ask if he did it on his own initiative or on the instruc- tion of the Watch Committee. Walker's Brewery Cricket Club. The annual dinner in connection with this organiza- tion was held at the Lion Hotel on Saturday evening, when Councillor J. Fulton-Smith presided over an attendance of over SO members and friends.—After dinner numerous toasts were honoured, and the Chair- man distributed medals and bats as follows :—Medals for the runners-up in the Warrington Works Com- petition : Messrs. H. Cook (captain), J. Cain, W. Norman, W. Ashton, W. Wright, G. Pearson, W. Baird, J. Titobard, J. Edwards, J. Molloy, J. Pearnley, I. Turner, E. Rogers, A. Holland, J. Milner, and J. H. Grainger. Mr. J. Cain won the bat presented by Colonel J. Reid Walker for batting average 1908, scoring 240 runs, with an average of 14.00. Mr. W. Norman won two gold medals, presented by Colonel Walker, for bowling average 1907 and dead heat in 1908. Mr. W. Holland won a gold medal, presented by Colonel Walker, for dead heat with W. Norman, season 1908, and Mr. J. Edwards a medal from the cricket club for bowling average season 1907. Messrs. B. Price and A. E. Ross won a bat each, presented by the cricket club, for batting average 1907 and 1908 respectively.—A capital musical programme was submitted, including songs by Messrs. Peter Bate, B. Bate, L. Aukland, R. Grounds (humorous), J. Lloyd and G. Johnson •, quartettes by Messrs. R. Grounds, J. Lloyd, C. Gelderd, and L. Ankland ; concertina solos by Mr. T. R. Ford (who also acted as accom- panist), and cornet solos by Mr. Winstanley.</p>
